/** Full translations siman 094 — YD001 editorial pass */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `That was inserted. Shach wrote we do not know how much came out from it; and see above siman 98 seif 5 if we do not know how much was absorbed; and Maharshal wrote if it is not clear to him how far it was inserted they estimate in the default manner of insertion — until the head of the spoon (Knesset HaGedolah in the name of Radbaz wrote they said so only for definite issur, but for doubtful issur they estimate only by emission. And he further wrote in the name of Temim De'im: where one lifted a splinter stick full of dish from the pot — one may say what left from the spoon's emission cannot be nullified in the dish he lifted with that spoon because there is no sixty in that dish he lifted; and after lifting he returned everything to the pot with the splinter stick itself — everything became nevelah and forbids the whole pot if there is no sixty against what he lifted with the splinter stick — see there):`,
    '1#ב': `They used. Milk in it; but if they used it in a kli rishon with water it is not counted ben yomo; but in a forbidden spoon, even if they used it in a kli rishon of water, it is counted ben yomo when there was not sixty in the water against the spoon, as below seif 6 and siman 103. Shach. And Taz wrote in siman 95 seif 3 it will be explained that also by pouring kli rishon onto two vessels, one of milk and one of meat, and they are soiled, there is issur like kli rishon itself; therefore if one also poured kli rishon onto a vessel soiled from meat it is considered as if he used that vessel in kli rishon. And Maharshal wrote: I am inclined to be lenient with kli rishon standing by the fire — as long as it is hot, even if it is not yad soledes bo, it is fit to forbid, for they made distance for kli rishon as in the Yerushalmi — end; but Taz rules like Beit Yosef that even in kli rishon on the fire it does not forbid if it is not yad soledes bo, and so is primary.`,
    '1#ג': `First. Shach wrote that Mechaber's view too is that the first view is primary, therefore he wrote the last view in the name of "some say."`,
    '2#א': `Times. Maharil's reason: initially sixty is needed against all the milk, for we are concerned lest a little milk remained; and when they removed it from the pot the entire spoon became nevelah from basar b'chalav, therefore the spoon must be nullified the second time. It seems therefore Mechaber specified "if inserted twice," etc., and did not write generally that many times require sixty each time — because he follows his view that we do not say noten ta'am only in basar b'chalav, for there each alone is permitted by itself; therefore when inserted twice there is no longer room to say noten ta'am, since the spoon was already forbidden, even if inserted many times two times of sixty suffice; specifically if not known in the interim; but if known in the interim the first was already nullified and the second time too was nullified in those sixty; and even though Beit Yosef's view seems below end siman 99 that if an olive of issur fell into sixty olives of heter and afterward another olive of issur fell — it returns and is stirred even min b'mino even if known in the interim — nevertheless here there is no reason at all, for there is sixty against the spoon and the spoon would be all milk, only from noten ta'am two times of sixty are needed (and even though min b'mino also does not impart taste, nevertheless since it can impart taste against it when not min b'mino it is forbidden, and this does not apply here — investigate, end); see end siman 92 what Taz challenges from here on what Maharil wrote in Pesach laws regarding hag'alah from kli rishon — see there.`,
    '2#ב': `That suffices. Shach wrote even regarding the issur itself that fell into the pot many times we hold one need only one time of sixty, as below siman 98 end seif 4 in the gloss.`,
    '3#א': `The spoon. Taz wrote we do not say that what was inserted is already like hag'alah, for hag'alah requires boiling that raises a boil; and for issur it forbids only if yad soledes bo. And Shach wrote that per R' Avraham in siman 95 seif 3, even if there was only water or other things in the pot, if the pot was ben yomo and absorption was initially through the body of the meat the spoon is forbidden, and so we rule: the spoon is forbidden to use whether with meat, milk, or other things, for the spoon is noten ta'am; and see above siman 98 seif 5.`,
    '3#ב': `Its day. Meaning from the time of insertion, even if it is not ben yomo from when milk was cooked in it, since it was ben yomo at first insertion it became noten ta'am and forbids afterward even b'dieved if it is ben yomo from insertion; but if it is not ben yomo at second insertion everything is permitted, for although noten ta'am, now it is noten ta'am lifgam and permitted; but certainly l'chatchila it is forbidden to insert it in anything even if not ben yomo.`,
    '3#ג': `In benefit. (Meaning the bowl needs breaking and the food is thrown in the latrine, but not before a dog even if the dog is not his.) Hagahot Ashiri at the end of Avodah Zarah wrote if he afterward cooked when it is not ben yomo he throws the pot's value into the river and the food is permitted; and Taz disagrees.`,
    '3#ד': `Fruits. Beit Yosef wrote it implies even a moist thing, as long as it is cold, is permitted — end; and Bach challenged, for in siman 91 it is forbidden to place a moist thing in an issur vessel, and he answered that here it is like b'dieved where it is impossible unless the vessel is broken (but it is difficult for me, for in siman 121 Orach Chaim wrote that an earthenware vessel used for issur cold, since hag'alah is impossible, is called b'dieved and rinsing well suffices; Orach Chaim wrote this and Shach brought it — specifically a vessel not subject to hag'alah such as cups gentiles drank from: a Jew may buy and drink by rinsing; but other earthenware bought from a gentile, if there is only doubt it was used hot, requires breaking — it explicitly implies even for dry use it is forbidden, for it says breaking is required; and here Rama does not disagree with Beit Yosef — it implies even a moist thing is permitted to use, and investigate). And Taz in the name of Taz wrote even hot water to rinse the head or to do other things not needed for eating and drinking is permitted, and so is the practice; and per this practice one may be lenient also if a pot was forbidden from basar b'chalav and after twenty-four hours they cooked food in it — one need not throw the pot's value into the river; and Rashal disagrees and ruled one must throw, etc.; and all this in a pot that was forbidden; but food that was forbidden must be thrown specifically in the latrine, not before a dog even if the dog is not his.`,
    '4#א': `L'chatchila. The difference between pot and spoon is in Beit Yosef, for the spoon absorbed milk at the time of its benefit, therefore even after it is spoiled it is forbidden l'chatchila like a pot that is not ben yomo; unlike the pot which did not absorb milk taste until after it was spoiled.`,
    '4#ב': `B'dieved. Shach in the name of Beit Yosef: specifically if he returned and inserted it in the type he inserted the first time; but if he returned and inserted it in the type absorbed in it initially within twenty-four hours of insertion — obviously it forbids; and all the more if he returned and inserted it in other things such as water and vegetables — permitted b'dieved even within twenty-four hours of first insertion.`,
    '5#א': `Forbidden. Behold Shach left Mechaber and Rama in doubt, for since here there are three noten ta'am: one entering the spoon, from the spoon to the water, and from the water to the pot — one may wonder why Mechaber forbids cooking meat in it, especially since he permits below siman 95 noten ta'am bar noten ta'am in cooking; and further: how did he write "since it was new," etc. — it implies if he first cooked milk in it in actual form it is forbidden to cook other things, and this is not as he wrote below siman 95 seif 3 that meat bowls rinsed in a dairy cauldron with boiling water, even if both are ben yomo, everything is permitted as noten ta'am bar noten ta'am; and on Rama too one may wonder, for he rules plainly like Mechaber, and it is not so even for us, for in three noten ta'am all agree; nevertheless it is not difficult — why should it not be permitted to cook even milk in it, since anyway one must establish one use for this pot, therefore one should establish meat use, for nevertheless more of the last taste remains primary; and it seems all the more if they used actual meat in this pot into which a ben yomo dairy spoon was first inserted — permitted to cook meat afterward; but after the first spoon insertion we would not permit the pot to cook meat l'chatchila, since anyway one must establish a use for this pot — one should establish from milk absorbed in it — end (and certainly a great refutation is on Beit Yosef and Rama, and it seems to resolve their words with difficulty: the case is that the spoons were ben yomo and presumably when ben yomo presumably a little grease sticks to it as in siman 95 seif 3 one must say clearly there was no grease stuck on it, etc.; if so it is actual imparting of taste, and nevertheless Rama holds b'dieved it is permitted as noten ta'am bar noten ta'am, for this taste gives to the pot and from the pot to the water — two, as stated):`,
    '5#ב': `Sixty. Meaning even if both are ben yomo. And Taz challenged per the first reason in siman 95 seif 3 on those who forbid: we are concerned lest the bowls touched the pan and likewise lest the spoon touched the pot body — what does sixty help, for the spoon still absorbs and there is a second taste; one must say he holds like the second reason there — concern lest the water was forbidden, etc.; and here there is no such concern since there is sixty.`,
    '5#ג': `And to forbid. Meaning even to cook other things in it. And Shach wrote one must read "in the type of the vessel" with bet, not "like the type of the vessel" with kaf; and the explanation: although the food is not forbidden, nevertheless one may not eat it neither with meat nor with milk, since one can eat it without that — it is like l'chatchila, and one should pour it into a vessel that is like the one that is ben yomo specifically (and Rashal wrote they eat from the pot with a new spoon).`,
    '5#ד': `Stringency. Taz wrote: consequently also what Shulchan Aruch wrote "if two spoons in a new pot," etc., that the pot is forbidden — this too is only a general stringency — end; and all this in food where the Torah spared Israelite money; but if they cooked water in a meat vessel and inserted a ben yomo dairy spoon, the water is forbidden as Rama wrote in siman 95 seif 3.`,
    '6#א': `From meat. Meaning from the meat itself, or he cut them with a meat knife finely; but if he cut in one place of them — see above siman 96 what the law is.`,
    '6#ב': `That absorbed. Maharam of Lublin's responsum wrote: if there is sixty against the meat absorbed in the onion, then even the onion is permitted even though it was not crushed and softened, and one need not separate it; but if he cut the onion with a forbidden knife, even with sixty one must separate the onions from the dish by straining, for we hold "possible to squeeze it" is forbidden — end; and see above siman 96.`,
    '6#ג': `The pot. Shach wrote Beit Yosef wrote at end of siman 103: however, where there was absorption from basar b'chalav cooked in it, it appears to all that when hot water was heated in it within twenty-four hours it is considered as if the issur was cooked again in it, and we count twenty-four hours from heating the water, for in basar b'chalav all agree noten ta'am — end; and it is not clear, for the poskim' reason for noten ta'am in basar b'chalav is that each alone is permitted by itself and when together they are forbidden, therefore the meat itself becomes issur — unlike other issurim; if so here, where the absorbed matter in the pot was already forbidden, noten ta'am does not apply; nevertheless for us who rule noten ta'am in all issurim there is no relevance here in any case — end. But it is somewhat difficult: why is sixty needed here against milk the pot absorbed if they cooked water in it within twenty-four hours — do we not say noten ta'am bar noten ta'am from milk to pot and from pot to hot water heated in the interim and from hot water back to the pot, as in Sefer HaTerumah? One must say it is certainly not known clearly that all taste in the pot went to hot water and it is not noten ta'am bar noten ta'am but a doubt; nevertheless if the pot is not ben yomo it is permitted in any case — if at first everything went to the water it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am, and if not it is noten ta'am lifgam; and if so, in other issurim why do we rule if one cooked water in a pot within twenty-four hours we consider it ben yomo from when the water was heated and we do not also say in any case whether the pot did not emit into the water or emitted — perhaps it emitted in less than sixty and that taste was nullified in sixty, for noten ta'am in other issurim is only rabbinic and a rabbinic doubt is lenient? One must say the rabbis enacted this since there is doubt how much it emits — we say in every place stringently and are concerned lest everything emitted and everything became nevelah; even though in siman 98 regarding pouring min b'mino it is permitted from rabbinic doubt — specifically regarding pouring we say so, for if not poured one could estimate; but here it is impossible to estimate in any way, therefore they enacted to estimate for the whole pot — investigate; and if water was cooked in an issur vessel within twenty-four hours, whether we say noten ta'am and need twenty-four hours from heating the water — see end siman 103.`,
    '7#א': `With a knife. Even if the knife was below and the meat above, we do not say "the lower prevails," for through knife pressure it absorbs much — so is clear in Orach Chaim; and Shach wrote: one may say why we say the place of shechitah is hot and a peel suffices even with a gentile's knife forbidden from grease, and nevertheless only a peel is needed — for there not all the animal is hot, only the place of shechitah alone; but a piece is entirely hot.`,
    '7#ב': `That he cut. Shach wrote this follows the first view in seif 1 which is primary; and even though part of it is hot, all of it is hot, nevertheless he did not carry his absorption throughout; therefore what Rama wrote in the gloss "against the knife" means the place that touched the meat; nevertheless certainly they spoke only when he knows clearly how far he cut; but from the default certainly the way is to cut with the whole knife and sixty is needed against the whole knife except the handle; and even though in siman 105 seif 7 they wrote Tur that if issur fell on a piece in the pot outside the gravy, removal suffices — one may say this case is different, for through knife pressure it spreads throughout; or that a default knife also absorbs from butter grease, and the like; or the case is fatty meat; and even though in siman 96 if one cut a radish with a forbidden knife it is forbidden only the amount of removal — one may say there is only radish sharpness; and even though some poskim hold radish sharpness is stronger than hot, so that even if the knife is not ben yomo they make it ben yomo for benefit — nevertheless for spreading throughout in cooking, hot is stronger.`,
    '7#ג': `Peel. Shach wrote: and even though in siman 10 and siman 96 and Orach Chaim siman 447 we rule a default knife is not clean — nevertheless the grease on it does not forbid more than a peel; and even though in siman 96 regarding radish it implies if not ben yomo and not wiped it forbids the amount of removal — one may say through radish sharpness and knife pressure they make what is in the knife's peel and on the knife's face ben yomo and forbid up to removal; and Taz wrote even in the first part, if there is sixty against the knife's place, nevertheless a peel is required; and see siman 105 seif 5 that whatever requires sixty also requires a peel; and likewise even if not ben yomo, if there is no sixty against grease on the knife, all is forbidden; and one who is lenient in this is only among the astonished — end.`,
    '7#ד': `Hag'alah. Shach wrote: that which is written regarding a ben yomo knife that hag'alah is required, and not so if not ben yomo — that is because it absorbed from basar b'chalav and is forbidden to use for meat or milk; meaning because it is not ben yomo it is permitted to use for other things such as fish and vegetables, as in siman 93 in the gloss; unlike ben yomo, for the knife became nevelah, therefore hag'alah is required.`,
    '7#ה': `Second. Shach wrote this is R' Avraham's own view; but Orach Chaim and Maharshal hold in solid matter such as meat and the like that is not liquid there is no difference between kli rishon and kli sheni — as long as yad soledes bo it is forbidden; and Taz wrote it seems to me to be stringent in solid matter even in kli sheni where there is further reason to be stringent, as here one may say through knife pressure; but where there is no knife pressure one may be lenient as Rama (Bach wrote in s.k. 5: if one cut hot meat with a dairy knife in kli sheni and yad soledes bo the knife is forbidden because the meat exudes in it, but the meat is not forbidden, for kli sheni has no power to exude the absorbed and absorb into the meat — end).`,
    '8#א': `Cheese. Meaning cheese fell on hot panada.`,
    '8#ב': `Peel. Shach wrote: one may say for siman 105, issur that fell on hot heter requires removal, for cheese does not enter so much; if so the cheese itself requires removal; and Taz wrote: one may say for hot meat in seif 7, for here there is no knife pressure, and further there is sometimes dry fat on it that is not visible, unlike in a bowl; and Shach wrote: that which a peel suffices here is in something completely dry; but if there is a little moisture we rule in siman 69 regarding salting in a kneading trough that hot meat in a hot bowl requires removal; and even though in siman 105 regarding food, if both are hot, even if both are dry, all is forbidden — nevertheless in a vessel it is lesser since the issur is not in actual form; and even though there seif 7 implies a vessel forbids even non-fat issur even without gravy — one may say although it is better that it exudes without gravy, nevertheless it does not exude more than a peel; and nevertheless investigate — end.`,
    '9#_': `Of the permitted. Taz wrote: in Mordechai the reason is because grease in honey is noten ta'am lifgam, and he concludes there: but if it were for benefit it would forbid; and Beit Yosef holds the reason here is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am, and there is a difference in Mordechai's reason: even if there were actual meat in the pan in which they cooked the honey, then it would be forbidden and not noten ta'am bar noten ta'am; nevertheless here it is permitted because it is noten ta'am lifgam; if so Shulchan Aruch should not have omitted Mordechai's reason — end; and Shach wrote it is clear the case is they poured it by pouring and the stream did not stop; otherwise obviously permitted as above siman 98 seif 6; if so one must say only the honey is permitted but the bowl is forbidden per Rama's gloss in siman 95 seif 3; and one cannot say it is nevertheless permitted per Mordechai's noten ta'am lifgam, for Rama wrote in siman 103 seif 4 that where there is no great loss one should be stringent that grease in honey is not noten ta'am lifgam; possibly the case is a beverage made from honey, where all agree noten ta'am lifgam; nevertheless from Mechaber's language it implies even the bowl is permitted; perhaps since here there is a double advantage — even if cooked much, many poskim permit eating them in a dip, and some hold grease in honey is noten ta'am lifgam — permitted per Rama even for the bowl; and see siman 103 seif 2 in the gloss.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `Language of the Tur, and likewise in Semak siman 214 and Sha'arei Dura:`,
    '2#_': `There in the name of R' Peretz, and likewise Semak in his name:`,
    '3#_': `There, and likewise Semak:`,
    '4#_': `That if it is already known, the issur was nullified in sixty by the law of se'ah terumah, etc. — Mishnah chapter 8 perek 5 of Terumot and as stated in the Tosefta there:`,
    '5#_': `There and there (°) and specifically a dry matter, but not a moist matter — Beit Yosef; and it is permitted to warm in it liquids to rinse the head, and the food that became forbidden is forbidden to give even before a dog that is not his — Rashal and Taz:`,
    '6#_': `There, since the spoon absorbed the milk at the time of its benefit, unlike the pot which did not absorb until after it was spoiled:`,
    '7#_': `But if you insert it within twenty-four hours of the first insertion into the type that was absorbed in it at first, it forbids — Beit Yosef and Rashal:`,
    '8#_': `Semak siman 214 (°) explanation: from the spoon to the water and from the water to the pot, and both are still permitted (°) nevertheless one may not eat it with meat or with milk since one can eat it without that — it is like l'chatchila:`,
    '9#_': `Tur in the name of Beit HaTosafot, and because it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am of the permitted element:`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `One who inserts, etc. — we learn from a pot that cooked, etc., in the siman that preceded.`,
    '1#ב': `They estimate, etc. — as stated in perek 3 regarding "in it itself," etc., as above.`,
    '1#ג': `If, etc. — as above in the preceding siman.`,
    '1#ד': `In a kli rishon — see above siman 98 seif 11.`,
    '1#ה': `And some say, etc. — as stated at the beginning of perek Keitzad.`,
    '1#ו': `And the view, etc. — for even though part of it is hot, all of it is not carried in its absorption throughout — so hold the poskim; and Orach Chaim wrote that even if one says it carries its absorption throughout, that is through fire like Pesach. And proof for all this from what is stated in Zevachim 96b, Abaye said it cannot be except, etc., and see above siman 121 seif 6 and what is stated above siman 92 seif 5.`,
    '2#א': `If one inserted, etc. — lest a little milk remained and when they removed it from the pot the entire spoon became nevelah, as stated in seif 3 and as stated regarding kachal 97b and as stated above 105b "milk is nevelah," etc.; and Tosafot there s.v. why, etc.`,
    '2#ב': `And it was not known, etc. — they challenged from one who passes a perforated pot through a vineyard, etc. (end of perek 7 of Kilayim and conclusion), and a breach in a vineyard fence, etc. (Bava Kama 100 and conclusion), and similar in many places, and we do not say the first was nullified; and in perek 2 of Avodah Zarah 73a we say the first is nullified first, and Rashbam and Ra'avan and Riv'a explained that in Avodah Zarah it deals with known in the interim — Mordechai perek 9 of Bava Kama on two pieces that fell into each other: if not known in the interim, nullification is needed against both; if known, not required — as in perek 5 of Terumot, se'ah of terumah that fell into a hundred and he did not have time, etc., and the Tosefta concludes there R' Eliezer b'R' Shimon: in what case? when he did not know; but if he knew and another fell afterward it is permitted because it already should have risen — implying to explain the first tanna; and so Rambam chapter 13 of Terumot — see there; and even though in the Yerushalmi they say knowledge sanctifies it and the rabbis say lifting sanctifies it — Ra'ash wrote in perek 3 seif 37 that in other issurim the rabbis agree with R' Shimon since lifting is not required; nevertheless he ruled it returns and is stirred — see there at length; and from Avodah Zarah there is no proof, as Tosafot there 73a s.v. when he came, etc., and in other places; and see siman 99 seif 6 in the gloss on issur that was nullified, etc., and what is stated there at length.`,
    '2#ג': `And some say, etc. — many poskim hold in absorbed matter we do not say noten ta'am, as above siman 98 seif 8 and as stated above siman 92 seif 7; and even though we do not rule so, nevertheless in such a case there is room to permit; and see siman 98 end seif 4 in the gloss; but if, etc.`,
    '3#א': `If there is sixty, etc. — as stated regarding the pot in Zevachim (96b) and perek 3 and perek 25; and if he cooked, etc.; and for us, the seam of poskim is cited.`,
    '3#ב': `But the spoon, etc. — and we do not say it should be like hag'alah, for hag'alah requires boiling as in perek 2 of Pesachim (30b) "we boil in boiling water," and kli rishon means it does not suffice with yad soledes bo, therefore it says "in boiling water," for by default kli rishon yad soledes bo suffices, as in Orach Chaim siman 452 — see there; unlike for issur where yad soledes bo suffices. Hagahot Shmuel David. And it means as stated at the end of Avodah Zarah and Zevachim 97a that every day it becomes purged for its fellow; but the rabbis disagree with R' Yehudah there, and likewise at the end of Avodah Zarah it is not established so; possibly for the reason stated.`,
    '3#ג': `Whether, etc., and even, etc., whether, etc. — and likewise in other things, for it became nevelah.`,
    '3#ד': `But permitted, etc. — as stated in Avodah Zarah 32a there, etc., and as Tosafot there s.v. and this, etc., and from there Peri Megadim, etc., and there (33) that one may put beer in them: (Lekut) But permitted, etc., as in Avodah Zarah 32a and there 33 one may put water and other liquids; and in Bava Metzia 24b and permitted in benefit, etc. — Semak siman 55 — see there (end).`,
    '4#א': `If, etc., and the spoon, etc. — as stated at the beginning of siman 93 and Beit HaGolah s.k. 6 and as Tosafot at the end of Avodah Zarah s.v. from here, etc.`,
    '4#ב': `Nevertheless, etc. — Beit HaGolah.`,
    '5#א': `If they cooked, etc. — Shach and Peri Chadash challenged Shulchan Aruch and explain Semag's words in one answer and as in perek 8 (111b) that noten ta'am bar noten ta'am is permitted; and even per Tosafot there that only "they were cooked" is not so in three noten ta'am — all agree; see Tosafot at the end of Avodah Zarah 76a s.v. daughter and s.v. from here, etc., and Chullin 108b s.v. that fell, etc., and Ra'ash perek 2 of Pesachim and Sha'ar HaPesach; and all the more Shulchan Aruch ruled in siman 95.`,
    '5#ב': `Everything is permitted — for without ben yomo it receives spoiled taste, and that which is not ben yomo receives noten ta'am bar noten ta'am.`,
    '5#ג': `In the type of the vessel — as should be read — Shach there.`,
    '5#ד': `And it is only, etc. — as stated above siman 95 seif 2 in the gloss; but to place in a vessel, etc., and there seif 3 in the gloss unless one, etc.; unlike Tosafot 112a s.v. the law and if the one is not ben yomo, etc.`,
    '6#א': `Onions, etc. — Tosafot Avodah Zarah 76a s.v. daughter, etc., and R' Avraham wrote in Semak, etc. (and see siman 99 s.k. 17).`,
    '6#ב': `And all the more in a pot, etc. — for it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am.`,
    '7#א': `Hot meat, etc. — unlike Rabbenu who wrote that a peel suffices and brings proof from perek 1 (8b) that a knife "peels to one side" because the place of shechitah is hot; and Semak rejected it, for there it is not hot except the peel near the place of shechitah.`,
    '7#ב': `Or if he does not know, etc. — as in Avodah Zarah 38b and Tosafot there s.v. if, etc., and as stated above siman 122 seif 7; and see Tosafot perek 1 of Chullin 8b s.v. one who slaughters must stand, etc., and so Ra'ash there.`,
    '7#ג': `Only, etc. — as Semak wrote there in the name of Rabbenu, that the case of slaughtering with a forbidden knife even when not ben yomo requires only a peel; and so Rashba; and as Rashi 112a explained per the first view. And with this one need not resolve Semak that even when all of it is hot it does not forbid from slight grease stuck to it except a peel; and as in perek Keitzad (76a) that salting is different, a little, etc., and this is "the amount of a peel."`,
    '7#ד': `The knife requires hag'alah — and likewise a knife that is not ben yomo, but then it is permitted to use for other things as above.`,
    '7#ה': `The meat requires, etc. — like a forbidden knife: the place of shechitah is hot in kli sheni only from grease, and as stated.`,
    '7#ו': `And the knife is inserted — as at the end of Avodah Zarah regarding a gentile's knife to use it cold even though cold does not absorb and rinsing suffices, as in perek 28 107b regarding a knife; likewise here if he cut with it cold; and likewise per the first view in perek 1 (8b) that in the place of shechitah it is cold nevertheless he peels because of knife pressure; but Rashba wrote that nevertheless the reason is also that the place of shechitah is somewhat hot combined with knife pressure; and this is what is stated here in kli sheni; and that at the end of Avodah Zarah is for established use in something cooked hot or to cut something sharp, as in end of siman 121.`,
    '7#ז': `And even if, etc. — as above; and in the place of shechitah it is cold but somewhat hot as above, and likewise even per the first view as above.`,
    '8#א': `If fell, etc. — Beit HaGolah.`,
    '8#ב': `And likewise, etc. — meaning even when the bowl is also hot; but Rashba wrote three distinctions: in dry matter it forbids a peel; in slightly moist, removal; if fully moist, sixty against the whole bowl; and Beit HaGolah.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [seif 1] One who inserts a dairy spoon into a meat pot, or the reverse — they estimate for everything that was inserted, etc., because we do not know how much came out from it. Tur. Beit Yosef. Levush. Shach s.k. 1. However, where it is known how much was absorbed — such as when it was new (or not a ben yomo) and one shook out an olive's worth of milk from it and afterward (within twenty-four hours) inserted it into a meat pot or the reverse — since it is known it absorbed only an olive's worth, one need not estimate except against the olive. Beit Yosef and so below in Shulchan Aruch siman 98 seif 5 — see there.`,
    '2#_': `(2) There. They estimate for everything inserted from it into the pot, etc. — this applies when the vessel is hard: one estimates only against what was inserted; but in soft food one estimates against all of it — e.g., one inserted a cold piece of meat into a dairy pot and not all of it entered the gravy, only part — one estimates against all of it. Orach Chaim general rule 28 end law 3; Maharshal; see below siman 105 in Biur, section 9; Zivchei Tzedek section 1; and see below siman 105 section 11.`,
    '3#_': `(3) There. In a vessel, what was inserted from it into the pot, etc. — it implies that if he says clearly to me "until here it was inserted into the pot" he is believed, and we do not say in this "a matter not dependent on him," etc.; and so Rashal Piskei HaGahot siman 42 and Orach Chaim siman 65. Bach in this section. Noda BiYehudah II Yoreh De'ah end siman 16. Chavot Ya'ir general rule 46 section 6; and so agreed in Zivchei Tzedek section 2, unlike one who doubted this and wrote that also in practice every day when a question of Orach Chaim law like this comes before them they believe the questioner until where it was — and no one ever doubted the matter — end.`,
    '4#_': `(4) And if it is not clear to him how far it was inserted, they estimate in the manner of one who inserts a spoon into a pot — that is, the head of the spoon. Rashal there. Shach s.k. 1. Peri Chadash section 1; Levushei Serad section 1; Chavot Ya'ir there. And this means against the entire spoon except the handle. Rashal Piskei HaGahot there siman 41. Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Beit Yosef section 4; Minchat Yaakov on Taz general rule 85 section 1; and he wrote this means the head of the spoon, which is the round part, not the handle — end. And what Shulchan Gavoah wrote in Maharsham section 1 to explain Rashal's words — it seems because he did not see Minchat Yaakov's words; nevertheless it seems everything is according to appearance, for sometimes the pot is full and necessarily part of the handle was inserted, and sometimes one can see on the spoon how far it was inserted from the moisture it absorbed from the dish; therefore everything is according to appearance.`,
    '5#_': `(5) And likewise if a woman said "certainly it was inserted" or "until here was the dish in the pot" to estimate with sixty, or if she said the pot was not yad soledes bo — she is believed, for a woman is believed even in issur; Orach Chaim Tashbetz IV Chut HaMeshulash siman 42. Erech HaShulchan section 3, unlike Rav Beit Yehudah I Yoreh De'ah siman 41. And so Yad Efraim on this seif that a woman is believed, unlike Rav Beit Yehudah — and so Zivchei Tzedek section 5: in practice every day we believe women whether in basar b'chalav, salting, or absorption of issur, since handling the dish is in their hands and only women are at home, and we never found anyone who doubted this — end.`,
    '6#_': `(6) There. They estimate for everything inserted from it into the pot, etc., and one must estimate with a good measure — by measuring: fill a vessel with water, then insert the spoon into it gently, and the water that overflows is the measure of the spoon. Orach Chaim general rule 37 law 3 end section 1; Zivchei Tzedek section 4; and see above siman 93 sections 3 and 4.`,
    '7#_': `(7) And if it happened they cooked a dish in a dairy pot and put a little milk in the dish such that there is sixty in the dish against the milk, and inserted a ben yomo meat spoon into that dairy pot — everything is permitted, for the milk was nullified since there is sixty in the water against it, and it is also as if absent, as if there were only water, and it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am. Therefore the posek must ask and investigate, for they asked him when inserting a ben yomo spoon into a dairy pot whether there is milk in the pot or not, and also when there is milk how much — for sometimes they put only a little; thus he will rule rightly. Birkei Yosef in Sha'ar HaYichud section 1; Zivchei Tzedek section 6.`,
    '8#_': `(8) And know: if one inserted a meat spoon or an issur spoon into a permitted pot — that the poskim wrote one must estimate for the entire spoon applies when it was inserted into the issur itself, where everything in the first pot was issur, e.g., only milk was cooking in the first pot into which the spoon was inserted, for then all it absorbed is issur and we do not know how much it absorbed, therefore we estimate for all of it. But if the dish was permitted cooking in the pot and milk or issur became mixed in it and the spoon was inserted there and the spoon was forbidden because of that, and afterward they inserted it into a permitted pot — then one need not estimate against the entire spoon; rather we estimate as if a portion of that dish was taken — if the spoon holds a liter, for example, we consider as if one liter of that issur dish fell into the permitted dish, then we calculate how much issur reaches that liter of dish. The analogy: in pot 1 they cooked ten liters of dish and ten ounces of issur fell in — each liter has one ounce; if one liter of dish was taken and fell into a permitted pot, certainly one need not estimate except against the one ounce in it, for it is simple there is no noten ta'am le'heter; therefore this spoon, even if we consider it all dish, one liter in that pot's dish need not be estimated except against what it absorbed. Rav R' Shlomo Amar z"l in Piskei Dinim 20. Birkei Yosef there. Erech HaShulchan section 2; Zivchei Tzedek section 7; and he wrote so is our practice to rule. And so above siman 93 section 8 — see there.`,
    '9#_': `(9) A barley dish into which they put one part butter in forty of the dish and stirred it with a spoon and inserted the spoon into a meat pot that does not have sixty against the butter put in the barley dish — the teachers ruled forbidden, unlike those who permit. Responsum Penei Aryeh Chadashot siman 48. Birkei Yosef there section 2; but Erech HaShulchan there wrote that per what all hold, the spoon forbids only per the calculation of one in forty, and if so the meat dish is permitted. And so Peri To'ar siman 95 section 1.`,
  },
  'kereti': {
    '1#א': `What was inserted, etc. — not what was inserted in the food, only what was inserted in the pot; even if outside the food, since it is within the steam of the pot it joins to forbid — see Peleti where I clarified the matter in his reasoning.`,
    '1#ב': `Within twenty-four hours — meaning milk; but if they used water or other things within twenty-four hours it is nothing, for it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am of the permitted.`,
    '1#ג': `Hot in part of it, etc. — and likewise absorption and emission in all of it. And that which is brought "if he cooked in part of the vessel" — hag'alah is not needed for the whole vessel — is because as its absorption so its emission; and see what I wrote in Peleti.`,
    '1#ד': `And the first view is primary — for "hot" we say, but it does not absorb in all of it; but for carrying emission from outside the insertion into the place of insertion we do not say. And further I wrote to distinguish: if it is hot in any case it absorbs and emits in the entire vessel; but if it is only cold and hot from insertion into the hot pot — "part of it hot is all of it hot" — we do not say both to heat all of it and to emit and absorb from all of it — investigate; see Peleti.`,
    '2#א': `If it was inserted, etc. — see what I wrote in Peleti: one need not be concerned with one insertion of a spoon absorbing an olive of meat and emitting, and the spoon absorbs an olive of meat and within the spoon there is noten ta'am from meat, and it emits again — behold here are two olives and one would need 120 olives, for an olive of meat and an olive of milk do not combine and do not nullify each other. Only two insertions is there concern: in the first insertion it emits an olive of absorbed forbidden meat and becomes noten ta'am, and in the second insertion likewise an olive of absorbed forbidden milk, for it absorbs and emits more than one — we are not concerned, and they are two olives of milk and require 120 olives. And see Peleti: many poskim hold absorbed milk does not become noten ta'am, for it joins what is in the pot and nullifies meat taste, and noten ta'am only when removing the spoon — then what is absorbed in the spoon becomes noten ta'am and what is in the pot does not combine since he removes from the pot; if so, on first removal the spoon absorbs and becomes noten ta'am and there is no emission here; but on second insertion it emits the absorbed amount — only one olive, and on removal it absorbs again but does not emit until a third insertion — behold two olives and 120 needed to nullify; this is with three insertions, not two — a decree lest there be three insertions, therefore they decreed each insertion needs sixty; and see Peleti that hag'alah is not difficult.`,
    '2#ב': `And it was not known — then it returns and is stirred; an olive of first insertion's emission with an olive of emission — unlike when known, then the first is nullified first and does not return and is stirred; and see siman 99.`,
    '2#ג': `Twice. And Shach's view is Rashi's, that we do not say noten ta'am in other issurim; if so there is no concern on second insertion, for it was already forbidden from basar b'chalav and is like other issurim; and Peri Chadash challenged and it is correct with him, for since the spoon absorbs from meat it again forbids milk and becomes noten ta'am — what of it that it was already forbidden.`,
    '2#ד': `That one time of sixty suffices — many authors hold in absorbed matter in a vessel we do not say noten ta'am, only in absorbed food, as we say "milk is nevelah," etc.; and it also seems when there are two olives of milk the Torah nullifies by majority, only rabbinically sixty, and this is only a doubt whether it absorbed and returned and emitted, and a rabbinic doubt is lenient.`,
    '3#א': `But the spoon is forbidden. Bach challenged Tur who ruled like Rashba above siman 93 that cooking is hag'alah — if so here too milk should be hag'alah. See Beit Yosef; and it fits that they strained themselves; for me it is difficult, for the spoon absorbs from meat and truly in the pot there is sixty against milk, so the spoon that absorbed milk — the milk is not noten ta'am, for the whole pot combines; but meat absorbed in the spoon immediately becomes noten ta'am — what of it that milk has sixty — nevertheless the meat is noten ta'am as in another large pot, and if so it is issur and hag'alah in milk will not help since issur was absorbed — investigate.`,
    '3#ב': `All this when it is a ben yomo: if absorbed from meat and within twenty-four hours inserted into milk and within twenty-four hours of that insertion inserted into a dish — even though from meat absorption twenty-four hours already passed, for milk absorption twenty-four hours did not pass and milk became noten ta'am and is forbidden, for we count twenty-four hours from the last time it became noten ta'am — Peri Chadash.`,
    '3#ג': `If it is not a ben yomo — and even per what is written siman 87 in Beit Yosef, even noten ta'am lifgam is forbidden; and possibly here since there is sixty in the pot we are not so stringent to say noten ta'am and forbid noten ta'am lifgam — investigate.`,
    '3#ד': `Permitted to place, etc. — but to heat hot things such as eggs and the like is forbidden; and see what I wrote so there is no contradiction from Pesach law that permits on the eve.`,
    '3#ה': `The issur and after the fact that they cooked — it is permitted — this one forbids until the benefit value goes to the Salt Sea; and see what I wrote.`,
    '5#א': `Not meat, etc. — and even though it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am, since it is a new pot and it is not known in which type it will be used, meat or milk, they are stringent l'chatchila without using it for meat or milk.`,
    '5#ב': `Like the type of the vessel. And primarily: if possible to eat without meat or milk fat they eat only like the type of the vessel; but if impossible to eat so, it is b'dieved and they eat with meat or milk, for this b'dieved of having no choice to eat without fat.`,
    '6#א': `Only against the meat — see Peleti where I challenged that one must estimate against all the onions that became noten ta'am from basar b'chalav — see what he answered there.`,
    '6#ב': `Not required, etc. Maharam of Lublin wrote the onions too are permitted; see Peleti where I left it in doubt; and for practical law it seems the onions are forbidden.`,
    '6#ג': `Against, etc. — and if the onions absorbed from basar b'chalav, Beit Yosef holds the onions are noten ta'am and sixty is needed against the onions even for Rashi; and Shach and Peri Chadash hold onions are like other issurim, for "each alone is permitted" applies only when permitted absorbs taste from issur and there is no noten ta'am — per Rashi; and so is primary.`,
    '6#ד': `Within twenty-four hours — then we count twenty-four hours from meat cooking, not from water cooking; what was emitted to water is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am of the permitted and is entirely permitted, and we are concerned only for meat absorbed that did not emit in water and needs twenty-four hours from that time; specifically if meat was cooked; but if milk was cooked and afterward water — per those who say noten ta'am in other issurim, we count twenty-four hours from water cooking for all; but per those who do not say noten ta'am in other issurim, Shach still raised that it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am of issur, and what milk emits to hot water is issur and from that time it forbids; see Peleti: we forbid only according to the measure of absorption, not more, for if we required against all absorbed milk one would need twenty-four hours from milk cooking.`,
    '7#א': `Against the place of the knife — from here it is proved Mechaber holds we do not say "part of it hot absorbs all of it" like Acharonim; and see Peleti there is no proof from here.`,
    '7#ב': `Place of the knife specifically — when he knows the knife's place that cut; but from the default we say he cut everywhere — Rashal and Peri Chadash; and nevertheless even with sixty one must be stringent with removal.`,
    '7#ג': `In a kli rishon — and Maharshal's view distinguishes between solid and liquid; see siman 105 what is written at length.`,
    '7#ד': `Insertion — and if he cut something hard, insertion is required even without kli sheni and entirely cold — Peri Chadash.`,
    '8#_': `To the oven, etc. — see Peleti resolving Tosafot's words per Beit Yosef — see there.`,
    '9#א': `And they poured — even l'chatchila permitted, for it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am.`,
    '9#ב': `Because it is, etc. — and likewise grease in honey is noten ta'am lifgam; he mentioned one of them — see there.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 94 seif 1) Inserting dairy spoon — estimate all inserted in pot. A.D.M. wanted to say not only in gravy but estimate in pot even outside gravy — per R' Yosi piece partly outside gravy emits throughout — same here spoon estimate whole pot even outside gravy; though to hurry distinguish piece vs vessel, hard to be lenient from own reasoning without proof — concern stringency. Gloss: intent not to be stringent only metal spoon part hot all hot; wooden spoon certainly not like piece — investigate. End gloss Maharash Cohen.`,
    '2#_': `(There) Some stringent: metal spoon, first view primary. See siman 121 seif 6 Taz Shach; Orach Chaim siman 451 Magen Avraham s.k. 4 — reconcile drop on pot spreads vs here Mechaber first view primary — for absorption we say absorbs throughout, for emission does not emit above insertion; though hot all over does not emit all — contradicts Rama siman 121 seif 6 partial hag'alah helps — why helps if absorbs all emits not all; or as absorbs so emits; how Rama here first view primary when drop on pot spreads; per Semag doubt spreads; terumah no pouring in part cook — heter absorbed only weak emission, forbidden absorption not noten ta'am even weak above insertion reabsorbs issur; Rama siman 121 partial hag'alah — absorb all emit not all; if above cold at absorption no hag'alah above; Semag doubt if spreads all vessels or only if all hot spreads, cold above no spread no absorb-emit — terumah "that" excludes even hot vessel no spread; if all hot spreads all issurim — fire pot drop milk stringent; here cold spoon no spread — Rama partial hag'alah when hot at absorption must heat at kashering then emits; cold above at absorption no hag'alah above — investigate.`,
    '3#_': `(Seif 2) Inserted twice needs two sixty. Shach s.k. 25 — initially sixty against milk, spoon became nevelah, etc. Question: issurim nullify each other — spoon emits meat to pot, absorbs, emits to pot — nullify each other; known vs unknown — only min b'mino no sixty, here no relevance known or unknown; seems always need sixty each insertion each nullification, not one for meat one for milk — issurim nullify; Gemara challenge two olives — spoon immediately emits milk, absorbs meat, emits meat — two forbidden emissions; if not known, each insertion needs sixty against meat forbidden in spoon on removal; second insertion why not third — all min b'mino; question pot meat milk covering — why not sixty against milk emitted; say distinguish vessel vs food as siman 92 — here vessel, Rashi even with sixty need covering — if no taste permitted per Mechaber below 99 — investigate.`,
    '4#_': `(Gloss) Some say one sixty suffices. Shach s.k. 6 — many hold absorbed matter no noten ta'am; not necessary — per siman 92 vessel absorption separated from pot contents joined always sixty against issur, spoon no noten ta'am, one sixty suffices.`,
    '5#_': `(Mechaber seif 3) All forbidden in benefit, fruits and cold permitted. Source Tur; Darkei Moshe: unlike Pesach vessels Orach Chaim 451 — there want to benefit from issur after Pesach; here different — Avodah Zarah 32: want permanence through other — forbidden in actual issur; bottles permitted — wine not absorbed much, actual issur; vessels absorbed more — no actual issur; if so why Orach Chaim forbid rent chametz vessels — there gentile uses hot chametz, actual issur, wants chametz permanence; here only cold use, no actual issur — permitted. Rama there permits heat chametz vessels for needs; here did not gloss — even hot forbidden; there not normal use; basar b'chalav even not normal use forbidden; how hag'alah pot basar b'chalav absorption — issur during hag'alah — forbidden to hag'ael basar b'chalav vessel until not ben yomo — resolves Tosafot challenge how hag'ael large cauldron — forbidden until not ben yomo; long gloss Maharash Cohen Vilna on Darkei Moshe — Magen Avraham siman 452 Rambam/Tosafot 43b eating required, Meiri forbidden from Torah not eating — gloss missed Rambam/Tosafot.`,
    '6#_': `(Shach s.k. 22) Ma'adanei Melech seems Rosh noten ta'am all issurim — Tur/Beit Yosef missed — I did not grasp end, matter clear as I explained; Pri Chadash wonders if no noten ta'am in other issurim what helps noten ta'am bar noten ta'am of issur — seems explains count twenty-four hours from all absorption in pot; if so what remained lifgam, what emitted when water heated — if all emitted not ben yomo anyway; if not absorbed whole twenty-four hours only seventh part issur reabsorbed — if cook need only sixty against seventh — not his intent; truly when heated only proportional issur reabsorbed, if cook need sixty only seventh.`,
    '7#_': `(Seif 7) Hot meat cut with dairy knife, no sixty against knife place. Shach s.k. 24 — though part hot all hot; per Shach s.k. 25 knife below — still not lower prevails, knife pressure absorbs much — only against cut place, no pressure where not cut — lower prevails.`,
  },
  'mechaber': {
    '1#main': `The law of one who inserts a dairy spoon into a meat pot. In it are 9 seifim: One who inserts a dairy spoon into a meat pot, or the reverse — they estimate for everything that was inserted from it into the pot (if the spoon is a ben yomo, that is, they used it in a kli rishon within twenty-four hours) (Arukh general rule 37). And there are those who say that if the spoon is of metal they estimate for all of it because part of it became hot and all of it became hot (and the first view is primary, and so is the practice) (Mordechai and Hagahot Shmuel David and Arukh) (and see above siman 98):`,
    '2#main': `If one inserted the spoon into the pot twice and it was not known in the interim, sixty is required twice (and some say that one time of sixty suffices, and so is the practice) (Tosafot HaRosh siman 183 and Hagahot Shmuel David and Arukh):`,
    '3#main': `If there is sixty to nullify the spoon, the pot and the dish are permitted; but the spoon is forbidden, whether with meat or with milk, because it absorbed from meat and milk; and even after the fact it forbids if they again inserted it, whether in meat or in milk, for as long as it is a ben yoma; and if there is no sixty, everything is forbidden in benefit, even the pot; but it is permitted to place into it fruits or cold items since one does not enjoy from the body of the prohibition:`,
    '4#main': `If the spoon is not a ben yomo, the pot and the dish are permitted and the spoon is forbidden l'chatchila, whether with meat or with milk; but after the fact it does not forbid since it was not a ben yoma:`,
    '5#main': `If they cooked water in a new pot and inserted into it a dairy spoon, and afterward they again cooked water in it another time and inserted into it a meat spoon, and both spoons were bnei yoman, and at none of the times was there sixty in the water — it is forbidden to use the pot, neither for meat nor for milk; but other things are permitted to cook in it since it was new and they had never cooked in it before: {Rama: However, if one transgressed and cooked meat or milk in it, it is permitted because it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am (Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 9). A pot in which they cooked vegetables or water and inserted into it a ben yomo spoon and the pot is not a ben yomo, or the reverse, or there is sixty in the food — everything is permitted; and they are accustomed to be stringent to eat the food like the type of the vessel that is a ben yomo and to forbid the vessel that is not a ben yomo (Arukh general rule 37); and this is only a stringency in itself, for by law everything is permitted:}`,
    '6#main': `Onions or vegetables that absorbed from meat and they cooked them in a dairy pot — if it is known how much meat was absorbed in the onions and vegetables, sixty is not required, only against the meat: {Rama: For it is not relevant to say the piece becomes nevelah since it is still entirely permitted; therefore one need only estimate against what was absorbed; and all the more so in a dairy pot in which they cooked water within twenty-four hours and afterward cooked meat in it — we do not say that one must estimate against all the water, only against the milk that the pot absorbed (meaning of Tur and Beit Yosef):}`,
    '7#main': `Hot meat that one cut with a dairy knife — the entire piece is forbidden if it does not have sixty against the place of the knife that cut the meat; but if it is not a ben yomo, or if one does not know that it is a ben yomo, it does not forbid except the amount of a peel: {Rama: And all this is with hot meat in a kli rishon; and then if the knife is a ben yomo and there is no sixty in the meat against the knife, everything is forbidden; and the knife too requires hag'alah; but if it is a kli sheni, the meat requires peeling and the knife is inserted in the ground (Arukh general rule 37), and so is the practice; and even if the knife is not a ben yomo one should peel the meat a little because of the knife's grease (Tur and Beit Yosef in the name of Semak):}`,
    '8#main': `If cheese fell into an oven of panada, even if moist, and likewise hot cheese in a ben yoma meat bowl — it does not forbid except the amount of a peel:`,
    '9#main': `If they cooked honey in a ben yoma meat pan and poured it hot into a ben yoma milk bowl, it is permitted because it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am of the permitted element (another opinion):`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(siman 94 Taz s.k. 1) Also by pouring, etc. It should not have combined them and taught them together, for their laws are not the same: by pouring we hold it forbids only the amount of a peel, and therefore one does not afterward need sixty but only against the peel; therefore Rama wrote that this means they used it in a kli rishon — on the Mechaber's words that we estimate for everything inserted into the pot:`,
    '2#_': `(there Taz s.k. 9) This too is only a stringency, etc. That is, for in Beit Yosef it is likewise in the name of Semak; and see what Shach wrote s.k. 15:`,
    '3#_': `(there Taz s.k. 13) He wondered at Bach and lengthened in his proofs, and all his proofs are nothing, for one who examines Bach will see he did not write so for practical law; on the contrary, for the truth of the matter Bach holds one always needs sixty, only he wrote so to settle Tur's mind according to Rif, for it seems so at first glance in his words; if so, what proof does he bring from Rashba and other poskim — behold Ra'ah in Bedek HaBayit there disagrees and explains that passage "how do they roast" without sixty and with sixty no peel is needed, as in Mordechai perek Gid HaNasheh, and so it seems at first glance from Ra'ash perek 3; nevertheless for practical law his words are true as we rule below siman 105 seif 5, and I too in Shach s.k. 28 when I wrote that the grease on it does not forbid more than a peel since it is a slight matter, etc., I guarded myself from this and did not write that even without sixty and if it is a ben yomo one needs a peel as Beit Yosef wrote — investigate:`,
    '4#_': `(there Taz s.k. 17) If so Shulchan Aruch should not have omitted, etc. It is difficult for me, for here this law has no place; and below siman 103 regarding noten ta'am lifgam they wrote together in Shulchan Aruch — see there:`,
  },
  'peleti': {
    '1#א': `For all that was inserted from it into the pot — it seems even into the dish he did not insert so much, only in the pot, for the dish does not reach the bottom of the pot; nevertheless one must estimate against all that was in the pot, for we hold like R' Yosi: if a piece is partly in gravy and partly outside, it emits and carries gravy throughout; if so, what difference piece vs vessel partly in gravy — carries taste and emits from all of it; rather emission from vessel is stronger than from piece, as explained below siman 105; and in a piece many authors hold impossible to squeeze out issur always inside, and vessel benefits from hag'alah — from this to distinguish and be lenient; therefore all in the pot forbids, but what protrudes outside the pot does not, for even R' Yosi who says gravy carries throughout — that is when pot steam assists and it is fully hot; but outside the pot there is no pot steam and heat to assist — therefore he parsed "all that was inserted in the pot" and not "in the dish."`,
    '1#ב': `And some say if it is a metal spoon one may challenge per dividers who say in metal part hot is all hot — why did poskim wonder about earthenware and a drop on a pot whether it spreads throughout, from "if he cooked in part of the vessel" needs pouring and rinsing in the whole vessel — there metal part hot all hot, and Temple vessel was copper; but in earthenware whence this — say like Rambam: except chatat, in all kodshim even earthenware needs pouring and rinsing; and what we learned "if he cooked in part of the vessel" applies even to earthenware — difficult; say plainly written in Scripture even in rinsed vessel where part hot all hot does not apply — investigate.`,
    '1#ג': `Regarding metal, the latter ones agreed Mechaber's law follows the first view: even in metal we estimate only against insertion, for part hot all hot we say, but not its steam; and I am very puzzled, for in the Gemara "if he cooked in part of the vessel" requires pouring and rinsing in the whole vessel — specifically kodshim, not terumah, as written "that" excludes terumah; with several reasons why in terumah not — perhaps permitted taste only weakened as siman 93; by this the law in basar b'chalav siman 92 "if cooked in part of vessel" means part of spoon inserted, not whole spoon outside insertion — no estimate outside, for in basar b'chalav permitted taste weak — but below siman 98 Mechaber brings these views and leans to first view: issur fell on spoon, we do not say part hot all hot — difficult, there issur absorbed and in issur we say pouring and rinsing in whole vessel; and above siman 92 drop of milk on pot we forbid until two times sixty from spreading — here we say does not spread, only weak taste — why forbid in 92 — stretch: before spreading place of drop became nevelah and spread, issur even weak taste forbids, difficult here too; also q: if absorbed in whole vessel when cooked in part, when cooked again in part emits whole vessel — why not fivefold measure whole vessel — also on Semag who rules forbid only inside, drop of milk spreads in whole vessel especially metal — investigate; perhaps say absorption spreads in whole vessel by nature of dish but to extract absorbed from absorbed in vessel emits only against dish not above — say no "as its absorption so emission," and terumah no pouring in whole vessel since no emission — perhaps if will cook elsewhere even terumah needs rinsing, only if cooks where it was, rinsing suffices; Orach Chaim siman 121 seif 16 for hechsher must kasher all, not say part hot all hot for emission — unlike Rambam as its absorption so emission — every place doubt absorption, kasher all — investigate Rambam. Who will grant I grasp Rambam end — he rules cooking in part helps hag'alah in part; why permit not estimate whole spoon if absorbs and emits whole spoon — primary as Shach as its absorption so emission — truly no absorb/emit — but above 92 drop of milk and investigate; Orach Chaim long general rule 58 and 57 rules metal spoon forbids only inserted, emits no more; hag'alah as its absorption so emission — seems emits more; Semak drop on pot spreads — contradiction needs study; stretch parse Orach Chaim: not estimate whole spoon though part hot all hot because heated by hot water not by its own fire, does not carry emission throughout — if vessel cold only inserted in hot water, part hot all hot we do not say heats whole vessel and carries emission — Orach Chaim intent; unlike pot by fire spreads; here cold spoon only hot from insertion — such heat does not carry absorption to emit in whole spoon; siman 121 whole pot hot from fire needs heat before hag'alah — principle as its absorption so emission all one pattern without difference — as its absorption vessel must be all hot, so emission; Tosafot Zevachim 96b bishul miktzat kli divides vessel all cold or all hot; Taz distinguishes cold vs all hot — Mechaber siman 122 does not seem to divide — seems holds part hot all hot is heat — investigate.`,
    '2#_': `If the spoon was inserted twice — to clarify: on second insertion what issur adds — from one insertion in meat pieces that fell in milk resting unknown time, milk absorbed and became noten ta'am and emitted again as Gemara "meat fell in large cauldron of milk" — permitted, milk is nevelah — similar here most of pot noten ta'am, especially unknown, we say returns and is stirred; must say we concern only emission like roasters first time, not absorb-emit repeatedly — wonder on Ramban/Ran end Kol HaBasar; same law spoon once: emits olive meat absorbed, olive milk absorbed this insertion — two olives, still one insertion needs two sixty? olive meat and olive milk nullify each other siman 98 end — two insertions: each end insertion olive forbidden milk noten ta'am, two forbidden milk olives combine — two times sixty; Raavan as siman 87 pot meat then milk permitted by covering — not nevelah then emit, joined with pot contents nullified, Tosafot large cauldrons hag'alah — small cauldrons with sixty not nevelah, joined with contents nullified — plain; spoon contents also join with pot nullified, not noten ta'am — two insertions permitted too; perhaps those who concern absorption noten ta'am do not hold all in pot joins — pot meat cooked milk rely on covering only if sixty — if noten ta'am nullified in sixty, covering per Rashi even with sixty need covering — for us not like Rashi, all in pot joins — question remains why two insertions two sixty; Terumat HaDeshen implies difference spoon vs pot — at removal from pot forbidden absorbed collects in spoon, pot contents do not join, noten ta'am at removal; second insertion emits milk absorbed at first removal — two times sixty; investigate; two sixty if one against milk one against meat emitted — teachers of Terumat HaDeshen and Maharil — great question as meat in milk needs two sixty one against meat taste one against olive milk, milk all agree pot contents do not join — Chullin "why permitted milk nevelah" — say as pieces nullify each other, spoon too — toiled, not settled, many answers vanity; Maharil responsum 161 Semag forbade by decree — understand what decree — if decree lest insert in another pot — very difficult, no such decree in sugya; if not known even if known forbid — we do not forbid from returns and stirs; say if three insertions forbidden as at first removal absorbed noten ta'am, second emits and at second removal absorbs olive milk noten ta'am, third noten ta'am emits — two forbidden milk olives — two times sixty; decreed two insertions lest three — each insertion sixty — most plausible of explanations; but difficult if concern Terumat HaDeshen at spoon removal absorbs milk becomes nevelah, pot not join — all hag'alah same when removing spoon from hag'alah absorbs from hag'alah — for us not like R' Avraham noten ta'am, spoon absorbed issur no hag'alah; Maharil responsum 161: what of hag'alah — per R' Yosi Chullin "why permitted milk" — waters forbidden by nevelah and noten ta'am — what relevance to spoon — they did not disagree if waters forbidden by nevelah what name — stretch: hag'alah extracts all absorbed without remainder; what newly absorbs at end of hag'alah does not emit, only what absorbed from issur emitted by hag'alah no remainder — if possible to squeeze forbidden though emitted permitted; Maharil: basar b'chalav noten ta'am from joining; other issurim noten ta'am from absorption — when issur separated returns permitted; we say possible to squeeze forbidden lest not emit — end hag'alah issur separated no noten ta'am; Maharil: basar b'chalav illustration — milk absorbed meat called milk, no meat here still forbidden; waters from nevelah if no nevelah waters permitted — no issur name only nevelah — Maharil per Tosafot waters named for issur — when separated not nevelah, hag'alah too — if so Tosafot spices in pot not taste-dependent — seemingly unrelated — resolved all depends if issur separated still noten ta'am; if noten ta'am forbidden cannot forbid — if separated not called by issur name, no noten ta'am — forbidden can forbid; Ran: also not called by essence — nevelah in bones; if separated no noten ta'am — forbidden can forbid; one pulls with colleague — investigate not known see siman 99; Shach R' Avraham two insertions — Pri Chadash disagrees, law with him.`,
    '3#א': `But the spoon is forbidden, etc. Beit Yosef wondered: Tur brought law without dispute, siman 92 brought Rashba for cooking is hag'alah — spoon too; see Beit Yosef; I did not understand their question — when milk enters spoon with meat absorbed, meat absorbed becomes noten ta'am from entering milk; even if milk sixty inside and out, they join per Magen Avraham — meat absorbed still noten ta'am, Rashba does not emit completely only weak taste — issur forbidden, here issur noten ta'am, basar b'chalav — therefore forbids — clear and correct.`,
    '3#ב': `Ben yoma — but if twenty-four hours passed, even absorbed from meat and milk permitted since lifgam; per my words siman 87 Rambam even noten ta'am lifgam forbidden — forbid even after twenty-four hours; perhaps if sixty in pot against absorption in spoon, many hold in absorption not noten ta'am in basar b'chalav — if applies, no need stringent lifgam; if less than sixty in pot, stringent per Rambam.`,
    '3#ג': `Permitted to place fruits and cold, etc. — seems not to heat hot; Orach Chaim siman 447 Rama permits even hot — perhaps how heat without cooking absorbed b'dieved basar b'chalav; see my words; Magen Avraham: permit heat because not normal use; basar b'chalav even not normal use forbidden — Rama permits only fruits, seems even earthenware — say occasional use only, fixed use forbidden — investigate.`,
    '6#א': `Or vegetables, etc. — law puzzling: Tur brought from Sefer HaTerumah — wonder how estimate only onion absorption; if onion three olives with one olive meat, need only sixty olives against meat — taste immediately absorbed in onion noten ta'am from meat — need three olives like Gemara "why permitted milk nevelah"; Sefer HaTerumah like Raavan in Mordechai Gid HaNasheh magenitza case — permitted Rabbenu, reason Sha'arei Dura absorbed does not forbid absorbed — drop milk needs two sixty, spoon two insertions two sixty — absorbed forbidden from forbidden does not noten ta'am; Tur like Raavan from pot meat then milk — all in pot cooks joined before noten ta'am — gravy outside maybe joins gravy in pot; if forbidden from substance not from absorbed like milk in meat — joins meat more than pot — noten ta'am; investigate; Terumat HaDeshen distinguishes food vs pot; Maharam Lublin permits onions with sixty in pot — wrong: milk in onion not noten ta'am since pot joins, meat taste still noten ta'am, forbidden onions with meat issur, pot emission does not help — many hold impossible to squeeze forbidden, Mechaber siman 106 — investigate.`,
    '6#ב': `That they cooked water in it — see Shach who expanded even R' Avraham's view; if cooked in issur vessel within twenty-four hours after water heated, even no noten ta'am, issur taste in water reabsorbs in vessel — noten ta'am bar noten ta'am of issur does not apply; Pri Chadash wondered — why count only from first absorption — his words unclear since emitted spoiled, if water stood forever issur taste forbidden all agree no sixty, now reabsorb return to first state — no sense; diff: pot absorbed two olives issur, heated 100 olives water — default pot contents not sixty against absorption if noten ta'am — if cooked again after heating, pot reabsorbs two olives need 120 to nullify; if no noten ta'am in issurim, only two olives issur in water — noten ta'am bar noten ta'am, need sixty only two against two, pot absorbs proportionally — second cooking has sixty against absorption, forbidden only minor cooking without measure; Tosafot for R' Avraham large cauldrons — first time yoreh emitted, second absorbed only proportionally nullified second time — plain; Rosh end Avodah Zarah needs twenty-four hours from last cooking — not sixty against issur only against absorbed proportion — holds noten ta'am, need sixty whole absorption; Ma'adanei Melech Rosh holds noten ta'am all issurim — Tur/Beit Yosef missed — clear as I explained; Ma'adanei Melech Rosh like Rashi not R' Avraham; Rama afterward cooked meat within twenty-four hours of first cooking — not forbidden from first cooking lifgam and noten ta'am bar noten ta'am — plain; Tur agreed Rashba hag'alah in earthenware.`,
    '7#_': `Against place of the knife, etc. — from here later authors learned Mechaber does not say part hot all hot for absorption unlike first view seif 1; even second view, all wrote reason knife pressure absorbs throughout; otherwise emission only at insertion place, outside insertion though part hot all hot no knife pressure — does not absorb; all the more knife below — lower prevails — plain.`,
    '8#_': `If fell in panada oven, etc. Shach challenged Beit Yosef: Tosafot fish on meat pot — fish did not absorb only peel; Tosafot whether cold fish hot pot or reverse; Beit Yosef learns even hot pot only peel; Shach: Tosafot plain or Rav upper prevails cold fish hot pot, Shmuel lower prevails hot fish cold pot; Beit Yosef has proof there Gemara not explicit, Rav from inference gave bread in basket "gives taste" — Gemara: not so, women lazy check poorly; if Rav for that said gives taste — he holds noten ta'am bar noten ta'am forbidden; "not so" answers Shmuel — not to challenge Rav from incident — clear; difficult either way basket case: if cold hot pressboard — cooked all forbid noten ta'am — what proof; say pressboard cold pot hot — difficult why "not so" for Shmuel — cooked all agree; say even so only peel, not like cooked — Beit Yosef has support — investigate; Shach correct both very dry no moisture.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `That was inserted. See responsum Noda BiYehudah II Yoreh De'ah siman 16: it is explained there that even if there is no recognition on the spoon how far it was inserted, if he says how far it was inserted he is believed, and we do not say in this "a matter not dependent on him," etc., for in a matter the person himself does he is not likely to say so, since anyway he knows there is sometimes a difference in the matter and he knows how he does it; specifically regarding one who slaughters who did not learn — if after learning he says "clearly I slaughtered well" it does not help, since at the time of slaughter he did not know there is sometimes stringency regarding delay of pressing — see there; and per his words it is difficult from siman 18 seif 4 regarding confused slaughter and Shach there s.k. 15 and Taz siman 6 s.k. 3 regarding a flaw above half the knife — see there and what Noda BiYehudah wrote there; and the proof he brought from R' S siman 94 in Shach that we ask, etc. — Shach has no mention of this at all; possibly he meant Shach s.k. 28 what he disagreed with Maharshal regarding a knife — see there; and he should have written from end siman 94; but even from there there is no proof for spoon insertion, for that case is different, since it is a rabbinic doubt when there is no gravy, as Peri To'ar wrote there and Peri Megadim brought. And see Peri Megadim in Orach Chaim in hora'ah customs section 2 letter 13: it is also explained that if it is not recognized how far it was inserted one may doubt whether "clearly to me" helps, since it is a matter not dependent on him — see there and investigate. [And see Knesset HaGedolah who wrote one must estimate against the part of the spoon inserted into the pot even against what is above the dish, for it is hot from the pot's steam; and Chavot Da'at wrote on him: although what compels this from the dispute of Rashi and R' Yehudah in siman 92 is not compelled, nevertheless there is room to be stringent as he wrote — see there; and see Peri Megadim in Mishbetzot s.k. 1.] See also responsum Chasam Sofer II siman 82 who wrote per Peleti's reasoning it seems there is no relevance, and so is the practice of poskim that Maharshal brought in Shach s.k. 1 — see there.`,
    '2#_': `In a kli rishon. See Beit HaTosafot s.k. 2 in the name of Rashal; and see responsum Chavot Yair siman 101 who wrote: if they boiled milk in a pot and it stood until after it cooled many hours passed and they emptied the milk from it, and the next day they cooked meat in it — if it was close after twenty-four hours from boiling such that yesterday at this hour it was still hot, more than lukewarm whose measure is like the heat of spit — it seems to forbid, for although it was removed from the fire, nevertheless kli rishon remained there and they made distance and a fence; and all the more regarding that kli rishon absorbs and carries — so much more what is in it; and it needs twenty-four hours after it comes to lukewarm temperature — see there; and see below siman 105 s.k. 5 what is written there.`,
    '3#_': `Primary. Beit HaTosafot of Rav Maharit who wrote in the name of Beit Yosef: even though part of it is hot is all of it hot — that is for absorption, but not for emission, for it emits only what was inserted from it into the pot; and there is a difference: if the metal spoon's handle was inserted ben yomo into a dairy pot, sixty is needed in the pot against the handle that was inserted (end); and behold Beit Yosef wrote he heard so from the author of Magen Avraham — see there; and indeed so in Magen Avraham Orach Chaim siman 452 s.k. 24; however Shach's view here s.k. 3 does not appear so, but also for absorption we do not say all of it is hot, for it does not carry its absorption throughout; and so below siman 121 s.k. 17: primarily as Maharam Mintz — "all of it hot" is regarding if the vessel became hot at one end and issur touched the other end it is forbidden, but not that if issur fell at one end it spreads to the other side; therefore if permitted matter was used in part of the other end it is permitted b'dieved — see there; and so in Solut LehMincha general rule 85 law 1: if the spoon's handle from meat was inserted into dairy food, even if the spoon was used without the handle within twenty-four hours, one need not nullify it at all b'dieved; and so I brought in Pesach laws siman 452 s.k. 40, unlike Beit HaTosafot in the name of Magen Avraham; but nevertheless the spoon is forbidden to use again until hag'alah — end; and see responsum Ahavat Shalom I siman 83 who expanded on this matter and brought that where the whole vessel is not hot — yad soledes bo is not in it — we do not say part hot is all hot; and if the spoon's handle or knife was inserted before kashering, sixty is not needed to nullify it; and if the whole vessel is hot we properly say it spreads throughout the whole vessel like a hot piece; and if the other side was inserted into food before kashering, sixty is needed to nullify it; except for kashering, part use suffices — see there. And Shach wrote there in the name of Orach Chaim general rule 37 law 2: specifically when hot from cooking; but when hot from fire itself it carries and emits its absorption throughout — see there; therefore if a metal vessel was heated by actual fire in part and part was inserted into the pot, one must estimate against the whole spoon, for all of it is truly hot and emits from everything.`,
    '4#_': `That one time suffices. And Sefat Emet Daniel 20 wrote: if it was inserted into a pot and there was sixty, and afterward inserted into another pot without sixty, and afterward returned to the first — two times of sixty are required.`,
    '5#_': `In benefit. Beit HaTosafot. And what is written "but not before a dog even if not his" — that is Taz's words who wrote so in the name of Orach Chaim and Maharshal and Taz; however the Gaon author of Mekor Chaim siman 348 s.k. 10 is not so, and so Biur HaGra there — see there; and some proof from below siman 134 seif 2 — see there and investigate. And see responsum Penei Yehoshua Even HaEzer siman 12 and Sefer Be'er Yaakov siman 29 on this matter.`,
    '6#_': `Since one does not enjoy from the body of the issur. See responsum Radbaz I siman 24. [And see responsum Chasam Sofer siman 98 who brought that to sell to a gentile a pot absorbed from basar b'chalav there is no business concern at all even on its day — see there.]`,
    '7#_': `It is kli sheni. Beit HaTosafot; and see in Solut LehMincha general rule 23 who wrote in Torat Eish he was inclined to uphold Maharshal's view; but see responsum Chasam Sofer siman 95 who expanded on this and wrote that in principal law there is not so much relevance, for we are accustomed to be stringent in all vessels even kli shlishi as long as yad soledes bo is in it, as Peri Chadash wrote in end siman 92 if not in great loss; and there are further views to permit; nevertheless if possible to kasher vessels and leave earthenware vessels to stand, we require kashering and standing, and sometimes also to boil kli sheni three times in boiling water after standing, as Tur siman 121 in the name of Ittur: whatever can be done in heter we do — then it means in great loss; and certainly when we do all the leniencies mentioned in great loss, even in solid matter Rama's view may be relied on, even in basar b'chalav where taste is like the essence it is stricter; nevertheless here it is certainly not full cooking as Minchat Yaakov proved end general rule 61, etc.; and all the more in other issurim that are not so strict everywhere — there is room to rely on the above. In principal law it is difficult from Tosafot Zevachim 96, etc., and so I received from my teachers the Gaon z"l to be lenient in solid matter in time of need — end; see there. And what he wrote that we are accustomed to be stringent in all vessels even kli shlishi, etc. — our teachers do not practice so; see Atzei Zeitim and Shach siman 105 and Peri Megadim there. And what he wrote at the end "and all the more in other issurim," etc. — at first glance it is not all the more; on the contrary in other issurim one should be stricter, for it does not depend on cooking method — there may be Torah concern — investigate.]`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(siman 94 Shach s.k. 60) Until the head of the spoon — that is, as it is round:`,
    '2#_': `(there s.k. 2) When there was not sixty in the water. But if there is sixty in the water we count ben yomo from use of the issur; and that which some say when they removed the spoon while it was boiling the water absorbed in the spoon became nevelah from the issur absorbed in it — nevertheless, since the issur that remained absorbed (and did not go out into the water, for what went out was nullified in sixty) is spoiled, we say the forbidden element is not stricter than the forbidding element, as Rama wrote below (siman 134 seif 2) in the gloss; but if there is no sixty in the water we are concerned something went out from the issur outside, and since the issur was outside the pot for a moment it is not spoiled; also according to what Rama wrote seif 2 that one time of sixty suffices, and it is explained in Terumat HaDeshen the reason there are two lenient views: one, lest when removing the spoon it is not a kli rishon to absorb milk taste from meat in it; second, Ramban's view that in absorbed matter there is no noten ta'am le'heter — if so, here too there is no issur from removing the spoon from the water:`,
    '3#_': `(there s.k. 8) According to the Rav, etc. This is astonishing, for if there is sixty in the water, for Rama too the spoon is permitted:`,
    '4#_': `(Shach m.k.) And it is simple. At first glance it is not simple, for the meat that became noten ta'am in the spoon from milk absorbed in the spoon — nevertheless the milk is spoiled (for what went out was nullified in sixty and what remained in the pot is spoiled), and since meat that became nevelah from milk does not forbid meat cooked within twenty-four hours (from the piece) [from the insertion] — perhaps Shach holds that for issur in basar b'chalav there is no "the forbidden is not stricter" for this; also, as long as the spoon is in the pot the meat did not become nevelah, and when the spoon was removed there are two sides to permit: perhaps the spoon is not then a kli rishon; perhaps in absorbed matter there is no noten ta'am le'heter, as Terumat HaDeshen wrote regarding inserting the spoon twice; and if so, combining these with "the forbidden is not stricter" there is room to permit in great loss — investigate for practical law:`,
    '5#_': `(seif 5 in the gloss) Like the type of pot that is a ben yomo. And if the pot is not a ben yomo and the spoon is a ben yomo, one may not pour from the pot into a vessel that is like a spoon while the pot is still boiling — investigate:`,
    '6#_': `(seif 6) And they cooked in a dairy pot. At first glance it would seem the precise wording is for that reason and he did not write "they cooked in milk" — the case is a pot of milk, but there is no milk in the pot only something else; thus sixty against the meat helps and the onions themselves are permitted, for there is no concern milk taste entered the onions and forbade them and would forbid the dish afterward if there is no sixty against all the onions, for milk taste in the dish before entering the onions is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am of the permitted; and if there is no sixty against the meat that is forbidden; even per Mechaber (siman 95 seif 3) here it is forbidden, for from onions to water it is not called noten ta'am bar noten ta'am since it was not absorbed in a vessel and did not become attached; but if they cooked in milk, one may say sixty is needed against all the onions, for milk entered them and became noten ta'am and went out and forbids, and the onions remain in their issur; but from Maharam of Lublin's words it does not appear so, and so from Shulchan Aruch gloss (siman 95 seif 2) — investigate:`,
    '7#_': `(Shach s.k. 23) Then even the onion is permitted. And if pickled in milk, one may say the onions too are forbidden — see Magen Avraham (siman 447 s.k. 138):`,
    '8#_': `(seif 7) The entire piece is forbidden. Rashba in Torat HaBayit (daf 125a) wrote: nevertheless, a knife, etc., and if one cut with it something hot or something cold and sharp like a citron — some say a peel suffices and some say removal is required, and this is Ra'avad's view — end; it is clear Rashba holds pressure and heat are one law with pressure and sharpness — investigate:`,
    '9#_': `(in the gloss) Requires peeling. Because of the grease on the knife; but when it is known the knife is clean, even if a ben yomo no peeling is needed — Taz entirely holds otherwise:`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["They estimate for all that was inserted, etc. — for we do not know how much came out from it; and see above siman 98 seif 5 if we do not know how much was absorbed; and Maharshal wrote in Orach Chaim s.k. 61 and Piskei HaGahot siman 41 that if it is not clear to him how far it was inserted they estimate in the default manner of insertion — until the head of the spoon — end."]`,
    '1#ב': `["That is, they used it in a kli rishon. Milk; but if they used it in a kli rishon with water it is not counted ben yomo; but in a forbidden spoon, even if they used it in a kli rishon of water, it is counted ben yomo when there was not sixty in the water against the spoon, as below seif 6 and siman 103; and see there."]`,
    '1#ג': `["And the first view is primary. For even though part of it is hot, all of it is hot — nevertheless it does not carry its absorption throughout, as most poskim hold; and see above siman 121 s.k. 16; and Mechaber's view too is that the first view is primary, as I wrote in this siman s.k. 28; therefore he wrote the last view in the name of \\"some say\\"; and see above siman 98 seif 4."]`,
    '2#א': `["If the spoon was inserted twice, etc. — the reason: Hagahot Maimoniyot on Chullin and Maharil responsum siman 161 and Terumat HaDeshen siman 283 wrote: initially sixty is needed against the milk, for we are concerned lest a little milk remained; and when they removed it from the pot the entire spoon became nevelah from basar b'chalav, therefore the spoon must be nullified the second time. It seems therefore Taz wrote if inserted twice, etc., requires two times of sixty, and they did not write generally; and if inserted many times, sixty is needed each time — because they follow their view that we do not say noten ta'am only in basar b'chalav, for there each alone is permitted by itself and through joining they become forbidden, unlike other issurim, as I wrote in siman 92 s.k. 10; therefore when inserted a second time there is no longer room to say noten ta'am, since the spoon was already forbidden; even if inserted many times, two times of sixty suffice; and Semak who holds noten ta'am in all issurim truly did not write in siman 213 this language that two times are required — see there and investigate."]`,
    '2#ב': `["And it was not known in the interim. For if known in the interim the first was already nullified, and the second time too was nullified in those sixty; and even though Beit Yosef's view seems below end siman 99 that if an olive of issur fell into sixty olives of heter and afterward another olive of issur fell — it returns and is stirred even min b'mino even if known in the interim — nevertheless here there is no reason at all, for there is sixty against the entire spoon and the spoon would be all milk, only from noten ta'am two times of sixty are needed (and even though min b'mino also does not impart taste, nevertheless since it can impart taste when not min b'mino it is forbidden, and this does not apply here — investigate, end); as Ramban and Ran wrote at the end of Avodah Zarah and Beit Yosef brings there, and this does not apply here — investigate."]`,
    '2#ג': `["And some say one time of sixty suffices. For many poskim hold that in absorbed matter we do not say noten ta'am, as below siman 98 seif 5; and so too in Terumat HaDeshen siman 283 that we rely on this reason; and even though we rule there stringently, nevertheless in such a case there is no reason to be stringent; and even regarding the issur itself that fell into the pot many times we hold one need only one time of sixty, as below siman 98 end seif 4 in the gloss; and Maharshal and Bach have other views here but we do not rule so; and see siman 99 s.k. 21."]`,
    '3#א': `["But the spoon is forbidden, etc. — see above siman 93."]`,
    '3#ב': `["The spoon is forbidden. Per R' Avraham in siman 95 seif 3, even if there was only water or other things in the pot, if the pot was ben yomo and absorption was initially through the body of the meat the spoon is forbidden, and so we rule; but per Mechaber there the spoon is forbidden only when inserted in a pot in which they are now cooking meat in actual form — see there."]`,
    '3#ג': `["Whether with meat or with milk. And likewise with other things, for the spoon is noten ta'am; and see above siman 98 seif 5, and in Magen Avraham there."]`,
    '3#ד': `["As long as it is a ben yomo. Meaning from the time of insertion, even if it is not ben yomo from when milk was cooked in it, since it was ben yomo at first insertion it became noten ta'am and forbids afterward even b'dieved if it is ben yomo from insertion; and this is clear, as Orach Chaim wrote in several places and Darkei Moshe siman 66 so explicitly, unlike Bach who wrote in seif 3 plainly the opposite — plainly an error; but if it is not ben yomo at second insertion everything is permitted, for although noten ta'am, now it is noten ta'am lifgam and permitted; but certainly l'chatchila it is forbidden to insert it in anything even if not ben yomo, since it became noten ta'am initially."]`,
    '3#ה': `["Everything is forbidden in benefit, and even the pot, etc. Hagahot Ashiri at the end of Avodah Zarah wrote if he afterward cooked when it is not ben yomo he throws the pot's value into the river and the food is permitted — end; and so Maharai and the Rav in Taz; and Maharshal in Orach Chaim and in his book Piskei HaGahot siman 46 disagreed and rejected his proofs."]`,
    '3#ו': `["Fruits or cold. Beit Yosef wrote it implies even a moist thing, as long as it is cold, is permitted — end; and see siman 91 s.k. 2 where I expanded on this."]`,
    '4#א': `["And the spoon is forbidden l'chatchila whether with meat or with milk. Since it absorbed from meat and milk; but with other things it is permitted, as above siman 93 s.k. 3."]`,
    '4#ב': `["But b'dieved it does not forbid, etc. Beit Yosef wrote: specifically if he returned and inserted it in the type he inserted the first time; but if he returned and inserted it in the type absorbed in it initially within twenty-four hours of insertion — obviously it forbids — end; and all the more if he returned and inserted it in other things such as water and vegetables — permitted b'dieved even within twenty-four hours of first insertion."]`,
    '5#א': `["If they cooked, etc. — until the end of the seif: in truth Mechaber did not intend well here, and the Rav too did not get to the point; therefore I must expand, for thus wrote Semag (siman 213 daf 103a): A case came before my teacher R' Yom Tov in a new pot in which they cooked water and inserted a ben yoma dairy spoon and there was not sixty in the water to nullify; and after a day they cooked other water in it and inserted a spoon used for meat, likewise ben yoma, and again there was not sixty in the water to nullify; and he ruled that taste was absorbed from basar b'chalav in a spoiled manner and the pot is forbidden to use for meat or milk, but to cook fish in it is permitted. It seems since the pot was new and they never cooked milk in it in actual form, even though a ben yoma dairy spoon was inserted and there is no sixty in the pot, nevertheless the pot does not become dairy thereby to forbid if they afterward cook meat in it the same day, for it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am: taste going out to the spoon, from the spoon to the water in the pot, and from the water to the pot itself — and it is still permitted. And likewise if after twenty-four hours from inserting the dairy spoon they heated water in it and inserted a ben yoma meat spoon, it is permitted to use the pot l'chatchila for meat, and milk taste absorbed in the pot is not considered taste, as explained. But truly as the Rav ruled, a pot absorbed from basar b'chalav even in a spoiled manner is forbidden l'chatchila for meat or milk; you find this if a dairy pot was used for milk in actual form and afterward meat was used — the meat is permitted but the pot is forbidden l'chatchila for meat or milk because it is absorbed from spoiled basar b'chalav — end (so is the law in Semag books printed in Krakow and copied in Kol Bo siman 106 daf 122b and Hagahot Shmuel David siman 85 and Orach Chaim Maharshal there and in Semag printed in Venice — the law is mistaken). Explanation: the case was they cooked water in it again after twenty-four hours, as he concludes \\"and he ruled absorbed from spoiled basar b'chalav\\"; and Rif challenges Maharai's ruling and says: it seems since the pot was new it does not become dairy, etc.; therefore why is it permitted l'chatchila for meat — milk taste is not taste; and the Rav's law that in basar b'chalav it is forbidden and other things permitted you find if meat was cooked in a dairy pot used for milk in actual form when not ben yomo; and so is clear in Kol Bo there explicitly, and in Orach Chaim general rule 35 explicitly, and it is plain. If so one may wonder on Mechaber why he forbids cooking meat specifically, especially since he permits below siman 95 noten ta'am bar noten ta'am in cooking; and further how he wrote \\"since it was new,\\" etc. — implies if he first cooked milk in actual form it is forbidden to cook other things, unlike below siman 95 seif 3 that meat bowls rinsed in a dairy cauldron with boiling water, even if both ben yomo, everything is permitted as noten ta'am bar noten ta'am; and on Rama too one may wonder, for he rules plainly like Mechaber, and for us in three noten ta'am all agree; nevertheless it is not difficult — why not permit even milk, since one must establish one use for this pot, therefore establish meat use, for more of the last taste remains primary; and all the more if they used actual meat in this pot into which a ben yoma dairy spoon was first inserted — permitted to cook meat afterward; but after first spoon insertion we would not permit the pot for meat l'chatchila, since one must establish a use — from milk absorbed — end. And certainly a great refutation is on Beit Yosef and Rama; it seems to resolve with difficulty: spoons were ben yomo and presumably grease sticks as in siman 95 seif 3 — say clearly no grease; if so actual taste, nevertheless Rama holds b'dieved permitted as noten ta'am bar noten ta'am — two times, as stated. And if water was cooked again after twenty-four hours, etc. — implies if within twenty-four hours from meat cooking it is forbidden even if water was cooked in between, and we do not say absorption weakened; unlike Tur in Rashba siman 93 regarding vegetables; Beit Yosef wrote no difference between vegetables and water; I wrote above to resolve. And Rama: if one transgressed and cooked meat or milk — permitted because noten ta'am bar noten ta'am. Pot in which vegetables or water were cooked and a ben yoma spoon inserted — everything permitted; they are accustomed to be stringent to eat the food like the type of vessel that is ben yomo and forbid the vessel that is not ben yomo — only a general stringency, for by law everything is permitted. Taz: although food is not forbidden, one may not eat it with meat or milk since one can eat without that — like l'chatchila; good to be stringent with food not to eat with meat or milk, but to place in a vessel of one of them no need to be particular. Rashal: practice is to eat from the pot with a new spoon; since only general stringency, practice follows Rama; nevertheless l'chatchila pour into vessel like the ben yomo one. Stringency: consequently Shulchan Aruch \\"two spoons in new pot\\" forbidden — also only general stringency; if water cooked in meat vessel not ben yomo and ben yoma dairy spoon inserted — water forbidden as Rama siman 95 seif 3. For us who rule noten ta'am in all issurim — if cooked water in pot within twenty-four hours, count twenty-four hours from heating water; three noten ta'am all permit l'chatchila even to eat in dip; Rosh chapter Kol HaSha'ah: even for those who forbid fish cooked in meat pot in dairy dip — here permitted, for taste from vessel to water and back is not considered taste when it returns to cook on Pesach; Tosafot end Avodah Zarah; Shulchan Aruch HaRav siman 75; Tosafot end Avodah Zarah daf Bat Yoma; Shmuel David siman 85; Mordechai Piskei HaGahot: if water heated in dairy pot within twenty-four hours not considered ben yomo from heating — even those who forbid cooked items agree in three noten ta'am; Hagahot Maimoniyot there; Beit Yosef Orach Chaim siman 452 in Shulchan Shel Leket: even for those who forbid cooked, agree in three noten ta'am — end; Orach Chaim divides general rule 34 between two and three noten ta'am; on Rama one may wonder who rules like Mechaber — for us not so even in three; nevertheless not difficult — establish meat use; all the more actual meat after first insertion — permitted; but after first insertion not l'chatchila for meat — establish from milk — end. Rif permits noten ta'am bar noten ta'am in cooking and rinsing when one not ben yomo; pot here stands on first matter; Rif after twenty-four hours not specifically — within twenty-four hours agrees to Maharai; truly as Rav ruled, etc. — you find dairy pot with milk in actual form, etc. Rif: new pot does not become dairy, etc. — why permit l'chatchila for meat; Rav's law — meat in dairy pot not ben yomo; Kol Bo explicit; Orach Chaim explicit; plain. Wonder on Mechaber; below 95 permits; \\"since new\\" implies milk first forbids other things — unlike 95 seif 3; wonder on Rama; for us three noten ta'am agree; not difficult — establish meat; actual meat after dairy spoon — permitted; after first insertion not l'chatchila meat — milk — end. Refutation on Beit Yosef and Rama; resolve: ben yomo spoons, no grease per 95 seif 3; actual taste; Rama b'dieved noten ta'am bar noten ta'am. Printed Semag mistaken case: new pot, water, dairy spoon; after days water again; \\"it seems pot new never milk\\" — not objection above but at beginning if second spoon within twenty-four hours; after all objections not resolved; plain Semag Krakow/Kol Bo correct; Venice mistaken; great stretch to say Rif holds noten ta'am l'chatchila within twenty-four hours forbids even other things — he permits noten ta'am bar noten ta'am l'chatchila; if dairy pot with milk in actual form why permit other things l'chatchila — say noten ta'am lifgam forbids l'chatchila — not noten ta'am l'chatchila; plain as I wrote; even Maharshal stringent on fish in dip permits here pot for meat after last insertion; Rosh Kol HaSha'ah; three noten ta'am; water taste not taste on Pesach; Tosafot; Shulchan Aruch HaRav 75; Tosafot Bat Yoma; Shmuel David 85; Mordechai: water heated in dairy pot within twenty-four hours not ben yomo from heating — three noten ta'am; Hagahot Maimoniyot; Beit Yosef 452 Shulchan Shel Leket; Orach Chaim 34 two vs three; Rama like Mechaber — for us not so in three; not difficult; meat use; actual meat permitted; after first not l'chatchila meat — milk. Investigate all this well."]`,
    '5#ב': `["And the pot is not ben yomo. For if it were ben yomo, pot and food and spoon are all forbidden, as below siman 95 seif 3 in the gloss; specifically when the pot absorbed initially the substance itself, but if absorption was through the spoon, as explained in the s.k. before this."]`,
    '5#ג': `["And they are accustomed to be stringent, etc. — this refers to the first law, not food where there is sixty — investigate."]`,
    '5#ד': `["To eat the food like the type of the vessel, etc. So in all books; incorrect: if so implies they eat it like the ben yomo vessel type — if vessel ben yomo dairy they eat with milk, if meat with meat — how then \\"only a general stringency\\"? Many poskim hold noten ta'am bar noten ta'am forbidden in cooking, as Rav brings below siman 95 seif 2; rather read \\"in the type of the vessel\\" with bet — \\"and to forbid the vessel that is not ben yomo.\\" Taz: although food not forbidden, may not eat with meat or milk — like l'chatchila; pour into vessel like ben yomo. Rashal: eat with new spoon; practice follows Rama; l'chatchila pour into ben yomo vessel. (Why not eat food like ben yomo vessel type — non-ben-yomo vessel gives noten ta'am lifgam — if dairy spoon not ben yomo and meat pot ben yomo, permitted l'chatchila with meat; Orach Chaim general rule 35; Rav siman 95; if vessel cooked or strained not ben yomo, permitted l'chatchila with other type — end. Maharshal stringent; Bach siman 66; no distinction spoon vs pot — noten ta'am bar noten ta'am forbidden l'chatchila in cooking per 95; where noten ta'am lifgam permitted l'chatchila — investigate.) But below siman 95 seif 2 Rav wrote fish cooked in meat pot permitted l'chatchila in dairy vessel — end; and gloss: meat bowls not ben yomo rinsed in dairy cauldron — all permitted; perhaps here only some are stringent and he parsed language in Taz \\"I saw teachers forbid vessel not ben yomo and eat food in type of ben yomo vessel\\" — most do not practice so; or since cooked in pot not ben yomo, if permitted in any vessel, concern lest err that ben yomo spoon does not forbid in water, etc., even if pot ben yomo — certainly forbidden per Rav s.k. 8; remote concern but practice explained; second question: since by cooking they are stringent more."]`,
    '5#ה': `["The food, etc. — specifically the food; but water, since not food, is forbidden as below siman 95 seif 3 in the gloss; so Taz there explicitly."]`,
    '5#ו': `["And to forbid the vessel that is not ben yomo. Even to cook other things in it — so Taz general rule 85 dalet; see in Magen Avraham why Mechaber permitted cooking other things."]`,
    '5#ז': `["For by law everything is permitted. And see below siman 91: dairy pot not ben yomo in which milk was cooked — forbidden afterward for milk or meat, for there absorption was in actual form; but here pot not forbidden, for what left the spoon is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am and permitted even per Rav siman 95 who are stringent l'chatchila on noten ta'am bar noten ta'am in cooking — as I brought s.k. 15 in Rosh's words and similar to rinsing when one not ben yomo. Nevertheless s.k. 15: Mechaber near \\"two spoons in new pot\\" forbids meat and milk — in this pot certainly forbidden to cook other type: if dairy pot, certainly forbidden to cook meat though not ben yomo, for l'chatchila forbidden; likewise milk, since meat spoon inserted — Mechaber forbade milk because spoon was meat. This case different: pot was dairy from start, not from spoon only — therefore worse. For law see s.k. 15 and investigate these laws in Epi Ravrebbi daf 61; my view written; see siman 95."]`,
    '6#א': `["Onions or vegetables, etc. — Meaning from the meat itself, or he cut them with a meat knife finely; but if he cut in one place of them — see above siman 96 what the law is.Tur wrote in Sefer HaTerumah: even in other issurim, a pot of issur that stood twenty-four hours without issur — if hot water was heated in it within twenty-four hours it is considered ben yomo, for issur taste in the pot emitted into the water and there was no sixty to nullify; we estimate for the whole pot and it becomes ben yoma and needs twenty-four hours after heating the water; but in basar b'chalav it is not so: if they cooked meat in it and within twenty-four hours heated water and afterward cooked milk — permitted even within twenty-four hours of meat cooking, for still entirely permitted; therefore only estimate against meat absorbed in onions; Semag (and Beit Yosef) reason: water in the pot itself is issur from noten ta'am, unlike basar b'chalav where still all permitted — noten ta'am bar noten ta'am; Beit Yosef end siman 103 for poskim who say noten ta'am only in basar b'chalav — this law not found in other issurim (see end siman 103 in Shach and gloss). But Tur wrote in Sefer HaTerumah generally and below siman 121 generally — he does not hold so; he ruled siman 92 we do not say noten ta'am in other issurim; rather other reason: noten ta'am bar noten ta'am does not apply to issur matter; Ran chapter 25: noten ta'am bar noten ta'am only where permitted cooked with meat in basar b'chalav — when second taste still permitted, since so much joined not considered to forbid; but in issur matter wherever taste exists it forbids forever — end; also clear there even for those who do not say noten ta'am in other issurim. See siman 95 s.k. 5; Shmuel David siman 85; Tosafot Avodah Zarah 76a end d.h. bat yoma — we count twenty-four hours from last cooking; Rosh end Avodah Zarah: if cooked therein, needs twenty-four hours from last cooking; Ma'adanei Melech: seems Rosh holds noten ta'am in all issurim and Tur/Beit Yosef missed Rosh — not so; matter clear as I explained; Rashba responsum 526 like Ran (and so Rosh chapter Kol HaSha'ah); Rashba holds noten ta'am not in other issurim; Beit Yosef siman 95 all poskim agree in issur matter noten ta'am bar noten ta'am does not apply; Taz general rule 57 plain from Ran's reason. Beit Yosef end siman 103: where absorption from basar b'chalav cooked together or alternately, all agree when water heated within twenty-four hours considered as issur cooked again — count from heating — end; he followed his view; not clear — poskim' reason in basar b'chalav each alone permitted — meat becomes issur — unlike here where absorbed in pot already forbidden, noten ta'am does not apply; for us who rule noten ta'am in all issurim no relevance — end. Difficult: why sixty here against milk if water cooked within twenty-four hours — noten ta'am bar noten ta'am milk to pot to hot water back to pot as Sefer HaTerumah? Say not known all taste went to water — doubt; if pot not ben yomo permitted — if all to water noten ta'am bar noten ta'am, if not noten ta'am lifgam; in other issurim why count ben yomo from heating and not say in any case whether pot emitted or not — perhaps emitted less than sixty and nullified, noten ta'am only rabbinic, rabbinic doubt lenient? Say rabbis enacted since doubt how much emits — stringency lest all emitted and all became nevelah; siman 98 pouring min b'mino permitted from rabbinic doubt — specifically pouring where one could estimate; here impossible to estimate — enact estimate for whole pot — investigate; water in issur vessel within twenty-four hours — noten ta'am and count from heating — see end siman 103. All the more below siman 96 Rama: only sixty against place removed."]`,
    '6#ב': `["That absorbed from meat, etc. — I would not challenge other issurim; this comes to exclude onions all absorbed from milk then meat — not enough sixty against meat alone; for us who rule noten ta'am in all issurim, specifically absorption from meat; Maharam of Lublin responsum: if sixty against meat in onion, onion permitted even not crushed; but if cut with forbidden knife, even with sixty separate onions by straining, for we hold \\"possible to squeeze\\" forbidden — end; see siman 96."]`,
    '6#ג': `["And in the pot. Meaning also in the pot it is only noten ta'am bar noten ta'am as we say; and see s.k. 22."]`,
    '7#א': `["Hot meat, etc. — even if knife below and meat above, we do not say \\"the lower prevails,\\" for through knife pressure it absorbs much — clear in Orach Chaim."]`,
    '7#ב': `["The entire piece is forbidden, etc. — and why do we say place of shechitah is hot and peel suffices even with gentile knife forbidden from grease, yet only peel — there not all animal hot, only place of shechitah; but piece entirely hot. Tur in R' Peretz; Maharshal Piskei HaGahot siman 59: difference regarding knife that slaughtered — issur from blood, the throat releases, absorbs little from much blood, peel suffices; unlike absorption of meat or milk. I did not grasp end of his view — plainly not like kosher slaughter knife where certainly no peel needed, for busy emitting blood does not absorb — as in sugya, explained siman 10; permitters compare to gentile slaughter knife — Chullin 8b even per place of shechitah hot, peel suffices; Semak siman 205 explicitly — end; here no blood issur, only grease issur — cannot distinguish; plain."]`,
    '7#ג': `["If there is no sixty, etc. — and even though siman 105 seif 4 Taz: issur fell on piece in pot outside gravy, removal suffices — here different through knife pressure spreads throughout; or default knife absorbs butter grease; or fatty meat; siman 96 radish only removal — radish sharpness only; some hold radish sharper than hot, makes ben yomo for benefit — for spreading in cooking, hot stronger."]`,
    '7#ד': `["Like the place of the knife. Continues first view in seif 1 which is primary; even part hot all hot, did not carry absorption throughout; Rama \\"against knife\\" means place that touched meat; only when knows clearly how far cut; default cuts with whole knife — sixty against whole knife except handle; siman 105 seif 7 Tur: issur outside gravy, removal suffices — different knife pressure; or default knife butter; fatty; siman 96 radish removal only — radish sharpness; some radish stronger than hot for ben yomo benefit — hot stronger for spreading. Maharshal Orach Chaim 41 and Piskei HaGahot 42: must estimate against whole knife except handle — not mindful how far cut; Rav and Shach primary; only when knows clearly; default cuts whole knife — sixty except handle."]`,
    '7#ה': `["Or if he does not know that it is ben yomo. Siman 10 s.k. 6 I proved Mechaber: if slaughtered with gentile knife, no difference — even if knows not ben yomo, peel needed for grease on face; Mechaber siman 96: even not ben yomo, peel for grease; Rav Orach Chaim siman 447: default knife not clean; grease does not forbid more than peel; siman 96 radish: if not ben yomo and not wiped, forbids removal — radish sharpness and knife pressure make peel and face ben yomo up to removal; Taz: even first part with sixty against knife place, peel required; siman 105 seif 5: whatever needs sixty needs peel; if not ben yomo, no sixty against grease — all forbidden; lenient only among astonished. Siman 10, 96, Orach Chaim 447: default not clean; grease only peel; siman 96 radish implies not ben yomo not wiped — removal; radish sharpness and pressure; Taz first part with sixty still peel; 105 seif 5 sixty and peel; not ben yomo no sixty against grease — all forbidden; lenient astonished — end."]`,
    '7#ו': `["And all this with hot meat in kli rishon, etc. until \\"and the knife requires inserting.\\" In Shulchan Aruch Orach Chaim long general rule 36 — not in printed Darkei Moshe. Behold Orach Chaim stringent: hot meat on strainer forbidden while very hot from kli rishon; Maharshal chapter 3 siman 44: kli sheni only water and gravy and liquids that mix and cool quickly; not meat/fish/solids — while yad soledes bo stays hot, exudes and absorbs — rice and porridge after pouring from pot to bowl, while yad soledes bo, like kli rishon for basar b'chalav: ben yomo spoon from meat, porridge absorbed milk — food and spoon forbidden from basar b'chalav, forbid another pot if inserted; if dish already forbidden from absorption, spoon not likewise forbidden without gravy; all the more if food milk or meat spoon not likewise forbidden afterward — end. Darkei Moshe siman 105 brings Rashal, disagrees: hot gravy holds heat more than thick meat — no proof; here lesser since knife pressure like radish siman 96 we forbid from taste. Prove from R' Peretz in Tur: some permit hot meat cut with dairy knife through peel like place of shechitah — who would be lenient in kli rishon with peel — if peel sufficed in kli rishon, how forbid from taste? Plainly kli sheni; nevertheless stringent sixty since yad soledes bo; if not, whole forbidden; Me'orei Or siman 105: hot meat with dairy knife in kli sheni yad soledes bo — knife forbidden meat exudes, meat not forbidden — kli sheni no power to exude knife absorption into meat. Stringent where knife pressure; without pressure lenient as Rama."]`,
    '7#ז': `["And the knife requires hag'alah. Written regarding ben yomo knife hag'alah required, not if not ben yomo — absorbed basar b'chalav, forbidden meat or milk; not ben yomo permitted fish and vegetables as siman 93 gloss; ben yomo knife became nevelah — hag'alah required. \\"Knife requires inserting\\" — even if meat soft, if cut hard thing like hard cheese, cold knife requires inserting as Orach Chaim general rule 58 dalet; see siman 89 s.k. 20; siman 121 inserting law."]`,
    '8#א': `["If cheese fell into panada oven, etc. — fell on hot panada; peel suffices for panada in removal; unlike siman 105 issur on heter hot needs removal — cheese does not enter so much; cheese itself needs removal."]`,
    '8#ב': `["And hot cheese in ben yoma meat bowl, etc. Binyan Yosef learned from panada: absorbed matter does not enter more than cheese; Taz: no knife pressure like seif 7; sometimes dry fat not visible on bowl; Shach: peel only in completely dry; little moisture — siman 69 salting trough hot meat hot bowl needs removal; siman 105 both hot dry — all forbidden; vessel lesser issur not in actual form; seif 7 vessel forbids without gravy — exudes without gravy not more than peel — investigate — end."]`,
    '9#_': `["Of the permitted element. Mordechai there on this law: grease in honey is noten ta'am lifgam; concludes: if for benefit would forbid; Beit Yosef: reason permitting is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am; difference in Mordechai: even if actual meat in pan where honey cooked — forbidden, not noten ta'am bar noten ta'am; here permitted because noten ta'am lifgam; Shulchan Aruch should not omit Mordechai — end. Clear case: poured and stream did not stop; otherwise permitted siman 98 seif 6; only honey permitted, bowl forbidden per Rama siman 95 seif 3; cannot say permitted per Mordechai lifgam — Rama siman 103 seif 4: without great loss stringent grease in honey not lifgam; possibly beverage from honey — all agree lifgam; Mechaber implies bowl permitted; perhaps double advantage — many poskim permit much cooking in dip, some grease in honey lifgam — Rama permits even bowl; siman 103 seif 2 gloss."]`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#_': `In a kli rishon within twenty-four hours. In siman 95 seif 3 it will be explained that also by pouring kli rishon onto two vessels, one of milk and one of meat, and they are soiled, there is issur like kli rishon itself; therefore if one also poured kli rishon onto a vessel soiled from meat it is considered as if he used that vessel in kli rishon. And Rashal Piskei HaGahot siman 71 wrote: it seems that kli rishon — as long as yad soledes bo is not in it — has no power to absorb at all; but I am inclined to be stringent with kli rishon standing by the fire: as long as it is hot, even if yad soledes bo is not in it, it is fit to forbid, for they made distance for kli rishon as in the Yerushalmi — end; but Taz rules like Beit Yosef and Orach Chaim that even kli rishon on the fire does not forbid if yad soledes bo is not in it, and so is primary; and see siman 105 seif 2 what I wrote on Rama's words in this.`,
    '2#_': `Into the pot twice. For it is as if two forbidden spoons were inserted; and if known in the interim the first was nullified and became fully permitted. In Terumat HaDeshen siman 283 he gave a reason to require two times of sixty: we are concerned lest the spoon did not first emit all the forbidden absorbed matter and part of sixty remained and permitted matter in the pot entered in parts and the absorbed matter in the spoon became forbidden and the whole became nevelah, and another sixty is needed against what renewed in the spoon from first insertion [that emits within the pot on second insertion]. And he rejected this, for forbidden absorbed does not forbid permitted absorbed; and he further sought a reason to permit one time of sixty. And Rashal perek Gid HaNasheh siman 40 was stringent to require two times of sixty — we have only the practice Rama wrote to be lenient, both in basar b'chalav and other issurim.`,
    '3#א': `But the spoon is forbidden. And we do not say that what was inserted is already hag'alah — Hagahot Shmuel David answered that hag'alah requires boiling that raises a boil, and for issur it forbids only with yad soledes bo; and further lest one err, etc.`,
    '3#ב': `Fruits or cold. Taz wrote in general rule 85 and Sha'arei Dura that even hot water to rinse the head or to do things not needed for eating and drinking is permitted, and so is the practice — unlike Mordechai perek Ein Ma'amidin that forbids using a moist thing; and he further wrote: per this practice one may be lenient also if a pot was forbidden from basar b'chalav and after twenty-four hours they cooked food in it — one need not throw the pot's value into the river as Maharai ruled — until here; and Rashal perek Kol HaBasar siman 46 disagrees with Rama, for the practice is not to warm but only to rinse the head in general, not water fit for any food and drink; and there is proof that rinsing the head is permitted even l'chatchila, etc.; therefore one must throw the pot's value into the river — until Rashal's words. And all this in a forbidden pot; but forbidden food — Issur VeHeter HaAroch and Rashal and Taz wrote one must throw it specifically in the latrine, not before a dog even if not his.`,
    '4#_': `B'dieved it does not forbid. Explanation: if they returned and inserted it in the type of first insertion; but if they returned and inserted it in the type absorbed in it initially within twenty-four hours of first insertion — forbidden, as Beit Yosef and Rashal Piskei HaGahot siman 47 in the name of Semag wrote.`,
    '5#א': `For it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am. From the spoon to the water and from the water to the pot — both times to the permitted element; for even the second time it is permitted by law, and what is forbidden l'chatchila is because the practice spread to forbid what is not ben yomo, as stated afterward — so Taz; and it seems the reason they were stringent in the pot here is because they place the food in a vessel like the vessel that is ben yomo; therefore it is not correct to permit the vessel that is not ben yomo since they pour from it, as Rama intended; and one may challenge Tur who wrote: and after it stood twenty-four hours from cooking the water, etc. — it implies if within twenty-four hours from meat cooking it is forbidden even if within that time they cooked water, and we do not say the absorbed taste was weakened; and this is not as Tur wrote in the name of Rashba siman 93 regarding vegetables that the absorption was weakened from the vegetables; and Beit Yosef wrote there is no difference between vegetables and water; and I wrote above to resolve this.`,
    '5#ב': `Or there is sixty in the food — everything is permitted. Even if both are ben yomo. And Taz challenged per the first reason in siman 95 seif 3 on those who forbid: we are concerned lest bowls touched the pan and likewise lest the spoon touched the pot — what does sixty help, the spoon still absorbs and there is a second taste; one must say he holds like the second reason there, concern lest water was forbidden, etc.; and here there is no such concern since there is sixty.`,
    '5#ג': `And they are accustomed to be stringent to eat the food, etc. Taz general rule 57 wrote: although the food is not forbidden, one may not eat it with meat or milk, since one can eat it without that — it is like l'chatchila; therefore it is good to be stringent with the food not to eat with meat or milk, but to place it in a vessel of one of them there is no need to be particular, as stated — end; and Rashal perek Gid HaNasheh siman 43 wrote the practice is to eat from the pot with a new spoon; and since it is only a general stringency, the practice follows Rama; nevertheless l'chatchila one should be careful to pour into a vessel like the one that is ben yomo specifically — so appears to me.`,
    '5#ד': `And it is only a general stringency. Consequently also what Shulchan Aruch wrote "if two spoons in a new pot," etc., that the pot is forbidden — this too is only a general stringency, for it is one reason with this law; and if water was cooked in a meat vessel that is not ben yomo and a ben yomo dairy spoon was inserted, the water is forbidden as Rama wrote in siman 95 seif 3; and see the reason I wrote there.`,
    '6#א': `Absorbed from meat. Meaning from the meat itself, or he cut them with a meat knife finely; but if he cut in one place of them — explained below siman 96 in Rama's words that only sixty against the place of removal is needed.`,
    '6#ב': `And afterward they cooked meat in it. Even within twenty-four hours of milk cooking; and that which is all the more so here is because here there is no absorption from milk itself, and the absorbed milk taste was already weakened by the water.`,
    '7#א': `Against the place of the knife, etc. Tur wrote in the name of R' Peretz: against the whole knife; and Shulchan Aruch did not bring it, for R' Peretz follows the view at the beginning of this siman regarding metal: part hot is all hot, and we do not rule so; therefore only against the place of cutting is required; but Rashal perek Gid HaNasheh siman 42 wrote one must estimate against the whole knife — the blade outside the handle, for everyone who cuts with a knife does not precisely measure how far he cut; and even if he says "clearly I only cut here" he is not believed, for every matter not dependent on him a person is not mindful — therefore one must estimate against the whole knife except the handle.`,
    '7#ב': `It does not forbid except the amount of a peel. Tur likewise in the name of R' Peretz; and from Tur's arrangement here it seems there is a textual error, and it should read: if it is ben yomo — if not, for presumably it is not ben yomo, since default vessels are not ben yomo — permitted, but a peel is required; and so in Hagahot Semak, and he wrote the reason is grease on the knife — default knives have grease on them. And it is difficult at first glance: in the first part, if there is sixty against the knife's place, why is a peel needed here if there is sixty against that grease on the knife — and what does a peel help if it carries taste throughout like the first part? Me'orei Or siman 105 wrote: if there is no sixty against the drop, all is forbidden even though the issur is slight; except it is clear that certainly there is sixty against the issur, or se'ah terumah in a hundred; but the difference is: in the first part, where the issur is from the knife's emission, one had to say it forbids up to sixty; but in the second part, permitted from the knife, only from grease on it, and it is a slight matter and there is sixty in the measure of a peel against it — therefore he did not need to mention sixty here since in any case a peel is required; and even in the first part where there is sixty against the whole knife, nevertheless a peel is required at the place the issur fell — slight in roasting where the issur is more attached than elsewhere; therefore that place must be peeled, and from this Rashba learned for every roasting where slight issur fell — sixty and also a peel; and so in Mordechai end perek Gid HaNasheh on Pesach that a peel is required even more than sixty; and so Ran perek Kol HaBasar; and so ruled Shulchan Aruch siman 105 seif 5. For this intent Tur wrote in the name of R' Peretz that a peel is required — meaning also in the first part where there is sixty against the knife's place; and he did not divide into two sections, but taught us: when it is not known to be ben yomo we go to leniency and sixty is not needed against the knife's place, but against the grease in actual form it is clear there is sixty and he did not need to mention it, and he concluded "but a peel is required" — both of them; and Shulchan Aruch that brought in the second part "it forbids only a peel" intended this too. I further saw in Hagahot Ashiri perek Gid HaNasheh on roasting requiring removal: he wrote there likewise what we said in perek Keitzad that grease on the knife is a slight matter, as in "salting is different, a little," etc. — end. There too he speaks after there is sixty; only concern from the place of falling in roasting here a peel suffices and full removal of the place is not required — so appears to me plainly; if there is no sixty against grease on the knife, all is forbidden, and one who is lenient is only among the astonished.`,
    '7#ג': `And all this with hot meat, etc. Taz wrote likewise, and I wonder that Rama is accustomed to bring Orach Chaim in all places and here did not bring his words, as in general rule 36, to forbid if one cut hot meat with a dairy knife that is ben yomo from hot use — through knife pressure it absorbs throughout and sixty is required against the whole blade, etc.; and one may say for kli sheni that in dry matter there are no cooling sides and it cooks; nevertheless all this while yad soledes bo is in it; and if there is no sixty the meat is forbidden even in benefit; and in kli sheni there are also sides of the vessel that cool it constantly even though there are no sides of kli rishon that hold heat long — nevertheless it absorbs; however Orach Chaim is not in the printed Darkei Moshe. Behold before us that even if hot meat is placed on a strainer, he is stringent to forbid the meat too — as long as it is very hot after coming from kli rishon; and so Rashal perek 3 siman 44: kli sheni applies only to water and gravy and anything liquid that mixes immediately and cools quickly; not to a piece of meat or fish and anything solid, for as long as yad soledes bo is in it it stays hot and exudes and absorbs well; therefore it seems rice and porridge too after pouring from pot to bowl — as long as yad soledes bo is in them they are like kli rishon for basar b'chalav, e.g. if the spoon is ben yomo from meat and the porridge absorbed from milk, or the reverse — the food is forbidden from basar b'chalav and the spoon too, and forbids another pot if inserted, or if the forbidden spoon the dish is forbidden from absorption even without gravy; but if that dish was already forbidden from absorption, the spoon is not likewise forbidden without gravy, since the food does not forbid from itself; and all the more if the food is milk or meat the spoon is not likewise forbidden to forbid another pot afterward — end; and Darkei Moshe siman 105 brings Rashal's words and disagrees: it seems to the eye that hot gravy holds heat more than meat and a thick thing; and it seems to me there is no proof from this, for here it is lesser since there is knife pressure and it is like radish at the beginning of siman 96 that we forbid from taste. And so it appears to me to prove from R' Peretz's words in Tur on this law: some want to permit hot meat cut with a dairy knife through a peel, as we say regarding the place of shechitah, etc.; and if he deals with kli rishon, who would want to be lenient in kli rishon with a peel — if in kli rishon a peel would suffice, how could one find in the world to forbid all of it from taste? Rather plainly he deals with kli sheni; and nevertheless he is stringent to require sixty since yad soledes bo is in it; and if not, the whole piece is forbidden; and Me'orei Or wrote in siman 105: if one cuts hot meat with a dairy knife in kli sheni and yad soledes bo, the knife is forbidden because meat exudes in it, but the meat is not forbidden, for kli sheni has no power to exude the absorbed in the knife and absorb into the meat. And it seems to me to be stringent where there is knife pressure; but without knife pressure one may be lenient as Rama.`,
    '8#א': `Into an oven of panada. Meaning a bowl of meat — they make in it a meat dish called panada.`,
    '8#ב': `It forbids only the amount of a peel. For there is no gravy there, and it is not like hot meat in seif 7, for here there is no knife pressure, only it rests on it; and further there is sometimes dry fat on it not visible, unlike in a bowl — so Beit Yosef.`,
    '9#_': `Because it is noten ta'am, etc. Mordechai there on this law wrote the reason it is permitted because grease in honey is noten ta'am lifgam, and he concludes: but if it were for benefit it would forbid; and Beit Yosef thinks the reason for permitting this honey is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am; and there is a difference in Mordechai's reason: even if there were actual meat in the pan in which they cooked the honey, then it would be forbidden and not noten ta'am bar noten ta'am; nevertheless here it is permitted because it is noten ta'am lifgam; if so Shulchan Aruch should not have omitted Mordechai's reason.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(siman 94 seif 1) One who inserts a dairy spoon into a meat pot — they estimate for everything that was inserted. From Maharshal's words Shach brought here: it implies if he says "clearly to me until here it was inserted" they estimate, even though when inserting he did not know it is basar b'chalav. From this Noda BiYehudah learned II 16: even though we rule everywhere "a matter not dependent on him" is not believed — that is when at the time of action he did not know there is stringency in this in any case, as in siman 1 one who slaughters who is not expert in slaughter laws; but if at the time of action he knew there is stringency in this in any case, when he afterward says "clearly to me" it helps even though it was not dependent on him at the time of action, as here. And it is difficult: here regarding a spoon Maharshal is lenient, and regarding a knife piece Shach is stringent in s.k. 28 in his name; and Shach is lenient there too and Peri Megadim brought Peri To'ar there: it is rabbinic and we say "a matter not dependent on him" in rabbinic matters. And it may be said they did not notice that regarding a spoon it is Torah law and nevertheless they are lenient, from the reason stated; and all the more one may be lenient regarding a knife. From this one may challenge what Tzitz Eliezer wrote in siman 65 regarding a woman who checked within five for her use and did not check again — we do not believe the examination for seven clean days, because "a matter not dependent on her" — Rav Meir Ayin Chacham and from above siman 1; and per Noda BiYehudah's view it is not so; and see what the son of Tzitz Eliezer wrote in Hagigah (19) that we do not say "not dependent" unless he did not intend anything — for me it is not so; and see what I wrote in Sha'arei Teshuvah; and so responsum Teshuvah Me'ahavah in the name of Kenaf Yonah that one should be lenient — the lenient who permit cheese after meat are fit to be lenient — end; and so common talk that household members prepare the evening meal with dairy even though they ate meat at noon and no one objects; nevertheless it seems for foods where the worker commonly eats during work there is concern because he is busy at work he swallows and eats issur — all according to circumstance; and if two workers know each other they ate meat — certainly do not be stringent, for they remind each other as we establish regarding reading by lamplight — end.`,
    '2#_': `(there in the gloss) One who inserts a dairy spoon they used in kli rishon within twenty-four hours — then all of it is forbidden. And it seems if used by pouring that did not absorb, only a peel — one need estimate only sixty against it. And I further found so explicitly in Shach siman 98 s.k. 11 and Orach Chaim general rule 37 in the name of Semak: even from doubt of boiling, sixty is needed to nullify it; and even if there is doubt whether all or part was inserted — sixty against all of it, for all cases and doubt is stringent.`,
    '3#_': `(there) And some say if the spoon is metal they estimate for all of it, etc., and the gloss: the first view is primary, etc., for even though part of it is hot all of it is hot — it does not carry its absorption throughout — Shach. And it appears to me primary as I wrote in Chiddushei perek Dam Chatat (96b) that it is clear in the Gemara that obviously it spreads throughout when he cooked in part, since all of it is hot; and nevertheless in other issurim it is not asked, for obviously rinsing and scouring of the whole vessel is not required as in kli rishon; and as Rama wrote in the gloss siman 121. But in kodashim it is properly asked whether we say so, for we see in several matters they were stricter than other issurim until the conclusion that truth is so, for the verse revealed to us "and if in a copper vessel it was cooked," even in part; otherwise it is impossible to resolve that passage at all, however much Tosafot wondered there; and so in Ran perek Kol HaShir; and I did not descend to the end of one of the Acharonim who wrote it carries absorption throughout but does not emit from all of it — so in Magen Avraham and so in Beit Avraham. Rather primary as from the Gemara: we say it carries its absorption throughout and emits from all of it. And it seems to say: specifically what absorbed through use of part of it — the general rule as its absorption so its emission; but what absorbed through use of all of it does not emit through insertion of part of it. And since there is no way to use so, we are not concerned presumptively. With this the Shach's challenge from Orach Chaim in Beit Avraham is resolved: a vessel forbidden from issur absorption that fell in part into a pot — one need nullify only that part; it deals where they did not use part only all of it, and in such a case it does not emit from all of it. But if we know that within twenty-four hours part was inserted into kli rishon, one estimates for all of it — investigate. Long afterward I found Peleti Mahadura Kama agreed with my resolution of the passage in perek Dam Chatat. And so agreed for practical law; only he wrote specifically when the whole vessel is hot — see there. Again I saw in Beit Avraham he disagrees with Shach that it does not carry absorption throughout, but wrote it carries absorption throughout but does not emit from all of it even when hot. It seems he did not see responsum Penei Moshe mentioned above.`,
    '4#_': `(seif 2 in the gloss) If the spoon was inserted twice, one time of sixty suffices. And it is simple that specifically if the same spoon was inserted, one time of sixty suffices, since there is no taste; but if another spoon was inserted, one must nullify both, for we rule it returns and is stirred if the first issur was added, as siman 99 seif 6 in the gloss; and see siman 98 seif 5 in the gloss.`,
    '5#_': `(seif 3) And if there is no sixty, everything is forbidden in benefit. What Beit HaTosafot wrote: even for a dog that is not his — so Orach Chaim general rule 21 and so Maharshal. In Sefer Machaneh Chaim siman 348 it is not so, and it seems he did not see Maharshal's words in Yesh Seder and Orach Chaim. And so in Hagahot Maimoniyot Laws of Chametz that burial is required specifically; and see Rashi Menachot (101b) that benefit is only for his dog whose sustenance is on him. And so Shabbat (175b) and Kiddushin (8b); and so inferred in Sefer Pe'er Yeshua from Rashi Pesachim (5) for the view that burning is not required specifically, only nullification in any matter — one may give to a dog that is not his. And in Sefer Orach Chaim he wrote Rashi cannot be upheld, for he holds chametz benefit is Torah law; but R' Eliezer who holds "do not eat leaven" is also benefit — he reads "leaven" for food for dogs. And his words are difficult, for Rashi there is for R' Akiva, as is clear there (23), and he holds like R' Abahu as Rashi explained there that he disagrees with R' Pedat; but even for R' Eliezer one may say he reads "leaven" to obligate the feeder, as in Yerushalmi; and this derashah comes for the view that the feeder is not obligated and also not to forbid benefit and also not for half-measure, as Rambam wrote.`,
    '6#_': `(there) Everything is forbidden in benefit, even the pot — for it is cooking and forbidden from the Torah, therefore forbidden in benefit. Erech Shai perek 7; if so forbidden l'chatchila to insert a ben yomo dairy spoon in a meat pot or the reverse, for it is cooking in basar b'chalav. And we say in the Gemara Piskei HaGahot (97) and Zevachim perek Dam Chatat (96) and so Taz siman 93: a pot in which meat was cooked — one may not cook milk in it. He speaks of actual cooking that is forbidden, for it is cooking milk within fat of meat emitted from the pot to the water. And do not challenge from what I wrote above siman 87 seif 6 that in absorbed matter in a vessel cooking does not apply — one may say that is where there is no actual matter, for meat and milk were both absorbed in the vessel; but where meat is in actual form and milk is absorbed, it is cooking meat within milk emitted to water, or the reverse. And one must say this is stronger than roasting a pot and types of frying that are not cooking, as in Solut LehMincha general rule 85 per Tosafot Sanhedrin — that only through water is it considered cooking, unlike where not liquid like water.`,
    '7#_': `(there) But permitted to place fruits or cold, etc. It seems "cold" is like fruits — dry matter entirely; but not moist. And as Tosafot perek Ein Ma'amidin (40) in the name of Rashi and Piskei Tosafot there siman 56 and Mordechai there and Beit Yosef and Be'er HaGolah — see there. With this Beit HaChayim's challenge on Taz why he does not require rinsing first is resolved, for I already wrote siman 91 that for entirely dry matter rinsing is not required even in earthenware — see there; but moist is forbidden to place in an issur vessel, and even rinsing the vessel does not help, only rinsing what was used in it — so wrote most poskim Rif, Ramban, Rashba, Ran, Maggid Mishneh, Kol Bo, Ra'avad — see siman 69.`,
    '8#_': `(seif 4) If the spoon is not ben yomo, etc. It is difficult, for noten ta'am lifgam from nevelah — we learn "give to a gentile" — and there it is permitted not in its normal manner of enjoyment; unlike basar b'chalav. And one may say it is compared to nevelah: what is forbidden from nevelah, etc., as in the Gemara.`,
    '9#_': `(seif 5 in the gloss) A pot in which they cooked vegetables or water and inserted a ben yomo spoon, etc., or there is sixty in the food — everything is permitted. And permitted to eat the food like the type in which it was. Darkei Moshe in the name of Orach Chaim. And it seems he means like the type of pot the food was in. And even l'chatchila, for the taste emitted from the spoon was nullified in sixty. And so there when he writes "when there is sixty" — see there. But to eat like the type of the spoon — certainly forbidden from the pot's taste; and although noten ta'am bar noten ta'am — nevertheless forbidden l'chatchila as cooking, as Rama wrote siman 95 in the gloss. And this is what the gloss nearby wrote: the practice is stringent to eat the food like the type of vessel that is ben yomo. It refers to the first dispute where one is ben yomo and one is not, not the dispute where there is sixty in the food; if so it should have said to eat the food like the type of the pot. And what is written to forbid the vessel that is not ben yomo — specifically regarding spoon insertion, as siman 95. In Sefer Chadashim he forbids the vessel that is not ben yomo even if there is sixty against it — unlike my view.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `If the spoon was inserted twice, etc. Be'er Heitev; see responsum Beit Yehudah siman 41 — ben yomo dairy spoon stirred in meat pot day one and two, then known, dish already eaten — doubt if sixty; per woman showed how far dish was, estimated sixty; answered per gloss siman 127: in doubt issur with sides to be lenient, woman not believed — light to be lenient; also Torah issur; further two insertions unknown — siman 94 requires two times sixty; say known from that spoon insertion not from homeowner — else plainly believed from migo could hide; even if others saw, wonder not believe woman — daily practice housewife or maid asks rabbi, rely if says permitted, not check lenient sides or doubt — therefore lenient: Rama doubt with sides to lenient — source Darkei Moshe from Ran Kiddushin 72b and Chullin — women believed even Torah issur except issur with lenient sides not believed; other issurim even much effort believed; Semag and Orach Chaim: definite issur like nikkur, gid — believed; doubt issur like fish among pure — not believed, woman light, did not check — from Orach Chaim; Rama: doubt maybe no issur needs clarification; or issur with lenient sides per Ran in Darkei Moshe — printers marked from Orach Chaim and Ran. Ran Chullin: women believed even Torah except where lazy like chametz check — only where issur not known can claim no chametz; other matters believed daily; Ran did not mention lenient sides — only unknown issur; if say Rama lenient sides means unknown issur — why split two clauses; seems lenient sides means legal sides not factual; Minchat Yaakov general rule 46 s.k. 32 on Taz — many lenient sides, Shach siman 84 wonders siman 127; distinguish many vs few sides; Rama means legal sides, split doubt and sides; wonder greatly: do women know law to fear lenient sides lie permitted — Ran Chullin never mentioned lenient sides — Darkei Moshe cited Ran Kiddushin "I searched" — not about women reliability; Pesachim Ran: because strict check, women lazy — if not rabbinic would not rely; other matters believed even Torah; seems laziness when issur unknown can claim none, check poorly; lenient sides not in words; wonder Darkei Moshe cited Ran words not found — unless Rama from index of Ran laws by R' Yosef Ottolang printed Rivoli 5319 in large Mordechai — I saw long ago, not now — likely Ottolang from Ran, missing word in print, should be "or issur with sides to claim lenient" — Ran: unknown issur women claim none, lighten; Darkei Moshe copied print, Rama split; parse lenient sides to claim lenient even no factual doubt; A.D.M.: precisely about checking — any issur not definite, can claim none, like lies no issur, not careful where great effort — Ran Pesachim laziness; asking issur/heter plainly rely when says permitted, no concern said permitted but was issur; even where concern arose like spoon case, per her words full with sixty — believed, permit on her word — main one witness believed issur we learn, rely on her mouth, rule on her words; sometimes rely even in checking with effort — investigate.`,
  },
};
